WEBVTT

1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
[dramatic music playing]

2
00:00:10,291 --> 00:00:12,458
You've seen the title across the screen.

3
00:00:12,541 --> 00:00:14,791
Yiya Murano is free,
and she's here today,

4
00:00:14,875 --> 00:00:16,458
joining us in the studio.

5
00:00:16,541 --> 00:00:19,458
And, of course,
I'm sure some of you might remember Yiya

6
00:00:19,541 --> 00:00:22,375
from those headlines
nearly 17 years ago

7
00:00:22,458 --> 00:00:25,541
that said "Yiya Murano,
The Poisoner of Monserrat."

8
00:00:25,625 --> 00:00:26,625
"YIYA" MURANO,
THE POISONER

9
00:00:26,708 --> 00:00:27,750
THE "POISONER"

10
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:28,833
THE POISONER'S CRIMES

11
00:00:31,625 --> 00:00:34,041
Yiya's here, telling us she's innocent.

12
00:00:34,541 --> 00:00:36,166
I haven't killed anyone.

13
00:00:36,875 --> 00:00:38,625
And I will say that until the day I die.

14
00:00:38,708 --> 00:00:40,583
-You've never killed anyone.
-I've never killed.

15
00:00:43,041 --> 00:00:44,666
[man 1] When you think about Yiya's story,

16
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:47,083
it's so unique
that one can only think of it as fiction.

17
00:00:47,166 --> 00:00:49,500
[man 2] No, the reality of it is
even stranger than fiction.

18
00:00:49,583 --> 00:00:50,791
A DANGEROUS FRIEND

19
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:55,500
Yiya, how do you think people can
actually believe you're innocent

20
00:00:55,583 --> 00:00:59,000
when the justice system
has found you guilty?

21
00:00:59,083 --> 00:00:59,958
You're on parole,

22
00:01:00,041 --> 00:01:03,500
and your own son says you've killed
two of your friends and a cousin.

23
00:01:03,583 --> 00:01:06,416
I've told you already
that my son has crucified me.

24
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,666
Martín Murano is the son of Yiya Murano,

25
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:14,750
who is popularly known as
the "Poisoner of Monserrat."

26
00:01:14,833 --> 00:01:15,708
[Martín] Mm-hmm.

27
00:01:16,625 --> 00:01:18,708
Every single thing I've ever done

28
00:01:19,208 --> 00:01:20,500
in my life

29
00:01:20,583 --> 00:01:22,583
was so I wouldn't be Yiya Murano's son,

30
00:01:22,666 --> 00:01:24,166
but just Martín Murano.

31
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,541
And it never worked out. It never did.

32
00:01:27,041 --> 00:01:30,375
What are the things
that you can't forget?

33
00:01:30,458 --> 00:01:32,666
Being the son of someone
who killed people.

34
00:01:34,875 --> 00:01:38,583
[man 1] Yiya wanted to be famous,
and she did it in the most gruesome way.

35
00:01:38,666 --> 00:01:42,416
There's a Yiya Murano who's
part of some type of legend by now.

36
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:44,166
-[Yiya] Yes.
-Who everyone thinks they know.

37
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:46,291
And they've made you into "The Poisoner."

38
00:01:46,375 --> 00:01:47,250
SODIUM CYANIDE

39
00:01:47,333 --> 00:01:48,416
MYSTERIOUS PASTRIES

40
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:49,500
CYANIDE AT TEA TIME

41
00:01:52,041 --> 00:01:55,583
Martín Murano, you've said
some truly awful things about your mother.

42
00:01:55,666 --> 00:01:56,916
I have.

43
00:01:57,708 --> 00:02:01,291
I thought to myself, "Why did he choose
to talk about me in that way?"

44
00:02:01,375 --> 00:02:03,791
I mean, has he forgotten I'm his mother?

45
00:02:03,875 --> 00:02:05,166
That I'm Yiya Murano?

46
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:06,458
[steady rainfall]

47
00:02:06,541 --> 00:02:08,208
[thunderclap]

48
00:02:09,791 --> 00:02:11,708
[man 3] For me,
it's one of the most unusual cases

49
00:02:11,791 --> 00:02:13,250
in Argentina's criminal history.

50
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:15,041
[thunder rumbling]

51
00:02:15,958 --> 00:02:18,375
I wanted to put an end to Yiya's story.

52
00:02:18,458 --> 00:02:19,875
What did I do instead?

53
00:02:19,958 --> 00:02:24,166
[woman] Let's welcome Mrs. Yiya Murano.
Come on out. Please, come in.

54
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:25,333
[Martín] I immortalized her.

55
00:02:25,416 --> 00:02:27,666
-Hello, Mirtha. How are you?
-Good afternoon.

56
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:29,416
Welcome to our show.

57
00:02:30,083 --> 00:02:31,333
[Yiya] Thank you very much.

58
00:02:31,416 --> 00:02:33,166
-[Mirtha] How have you been?
-[Yiya] Well.

59
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:35,083
[Mirtha] Well, please take a seat.

60
00:02:35,166 --> 00:02:39,916
YIYA MURANO
DEATH AT TEA TIME

61
00:02:42,166 --> 00:02:44,166
[mysterious music playing]

62
00:02:48,458 --> 00:02:50,750
[Mirtha] How are you all doing?
Good? That's great!

63
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:53,916
Next Friday,
we will have a very special show for you.

64
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,958
We have invited five mothers,

65
00:02:56,041 --> 00:02:59,416
who, with their love, sacrifice,
and dedication will represent all mothers.

66
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:00,500
MARCH 24

67
00:03:00,583 --> 00:03:04,250
It will be a show where affection
and care will take center stage.

68
00:03:04,333 --> 00:03:06,541
Today we have a brown linen tablecloth,

69
00:03:06,625 --> 00:03:09,166
decorated with white
Beauvais stitch embroidery.

70
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:10,875
The napkins, a light chestnut color.

71
00:03:10,958 --> 00:03:13,500
The centerpiece is made
of two-tone croton,

72
00:03:13,583 --> 00:03:16,166
feathery ferns, and yellow chrysanthemums.

73
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:18,833
And the bouquets are made with roses.

74
00:03:18,916 --> 00:03:21,583
Beautiful and pink.

75
00:03:22,916 --> 00:03:24,833
Stunning. Very well.

76
00:03:25,333 --> 00:03:28,208
Now, let me tell you about my outfit.

77
00:03:28,291 --> 00:03:32,375
Today, I'm wearing a white silk dress
with printed designs in blue.

78
00:03:32,458 --> 00:03:33,750
It's quite beautiful.

79
00:03:33,833 --> 00:03:38,083
An exclusive design by Marilú Bragance,
to whom we give special thanks.

80
00:03:38,166 --> 00:03:40,750
The shoes I'm wearing today
are blue sandals,

81
00:03:40,833 --> 00:03:44,083
in the same color as the print
of the dress, as you can see.

82
00:03:44,166 --> 00:03:45,958
Some light makeup.

83
00:03:46,041 --> 00:03:48,250
Hairstyle by Roberto Giordano.

84
00:03:48,333 --> 00:03:51,416
And now, ladies and gentlemen,
please, if you would be so kind,

85
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:54,458
join me in welcoming
our first guest, Mrs. Silvia.

86
00:03:54,541 --> 00:03:56,416
-How are you, my dear?
-[door closes]

87
00:03:57,291 --> 00:03:58,791
[dog barking]

88
00:03:58,875 --> 00:04:00,291
[woman] Mema? Mema!

89
00:04:00,375 --> 00:04:03,583
Mema, are you okay?
What's wrong? Mema, wake up!

90
00:04:03,666 --> 00:04:05,250
Pepe! Help me!

91
00:04:07,208 --> 00:04:10,291
Pepe! Pepe! Pepe!

92
00:04:13,291 --> 00:04:14,375
[man] Have a seat.

93
00:04:16,541 --> 00:04:20,500
I'm Horacio Romeo, retired officer
from the Argentine Federal Police.

94
00:04:20,583 --> 00:04:24,291
I was in the Homicide Division.
I was in charge of the judicial sector.

95
00:04:24,375 --> 00:04:27,916
At the time, I was serving as the judicial
officer when the judge on duty called,

96
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,708
asking me to come to the office.

97
00:04:29,791 --> 00:04:32,291
A new case had come in,
and he wanted to brief me on the details.

98
00:04:32,375 --> 00:04:34,375
NATIONAL CRIMINAL TRIAL COURT

99
00:04:38,958 --> 00:04:40,500
[Horacio] It was a suspicious death,

100
00:04:40,583 --> 00:04:43,833
but it had nothing
that was really out of the ordinary.

101
00:04:43,916 --> 00:04:44,791
SUSPICIOUS DEATH

102
00:04:44,875 --> 00:04:46,541
Pepe! Mema!

103
00:04:47,458 --> 00:04:49,833
-[Horacio] Mrs. Venturini felt suffocated.
-I just found her!

104
00:04:49,916 --> 00:04:52,250
-She went outside and lost consciousness.
-Doctor!

105
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:54,500
-The neighbors intervened.
-Doctor!

106
00:04:55,083 --> 00:04:56,000
-Come here!
-Hurry up!

107
00:04:56,083 --> 00:04:59,333
One of the neighbors, a doctor, even
gave her mouth-to-mouth resuscitation

108
00:04:59,416 --> 00:05:00,916
and a cardiac massage

109
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,833
while they called an ambulance
from a nearby hospital.

110
00:05:03,916 --> 00:05:05,750
-[doctor] Call an ambulance!
-Pepe! Hurry!

111
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:08,208
It's something quite common
in a sudden death.

112
00:05:08,291 --> 00:05:10,958
-Mema, please, wake up.
-[Horacio] Non-traumatic cardiac arrest.

113
00:05:11,041 --> 00:05:12,125
Wake up, please!

114
00:05:12,208 --> 00:05:16,083
And the autopsy certified
it had been a natural death.

115
00:05:16,166 --> 00:05:17,958
THE AUTOPSY
CONCLUSIONS

116
00:05:18,041 --> 00:05:20,666
ACUTE DILATED CARDIOMYOPATHY
ACUTE PULMONARY EDEMA

117
00:05:21,291 --> 00:05:25,333
The truth is that I began an investigation
in the Homicide Division,

118
00:05:25,416 --> 00:05:29,250
but without any actual expectations.

119
00:05:29,333 --> 00:05:31,166
[knocking at door]

120
00:05:32,458 --> 00:05:33,750
[man] Excuse me.

121
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:35,583
[young Horacio] Please, sit.

122
00:05:36,958 --> 00:05:38,208
[woman] Thank you.

123
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:41,958
[Horacio] All right. Name, please?

124
00:05:42,041 --> 00:05:43,458
César Augusto Caso.

125
00:05:44,666 --> 00:05:45,666
OCCUPATION: DOCTOR

126
00:05:47,833 --> 00:05:48,833
OCCUPATION: SUPER

127
00:05:51,875 --> 00:05:53,000
A NEIGHBOR

128
00:05:54,875 --> 00:05:57,916
I was cooking,
and I heard a horrible noise outside.

129
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,041
And so at one point…

130
00:05:59,125 --> 00:06:01,250
[Horacio] Everyone said
it had been something normal.

131
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:03,208
That she had fallen and hurt herself.

132
00:06:03,291 --> 00:06:05,625
That's when I saw Mema,
who was unconscious.

133
00:06:05,708 --> 00:06:10,333
There was no suspicion of a crime
or anything that might've suggested one.

134
00:06:12,416 --> 00:06:14,458
But the judge asked me
to summon her daughter.

135
00:06:14,541 --> 00:06:15,833
-See what she said.
-Name, please?

136
00:06:15,916 --> 00:06:17,250
Diana María Venturini.

137
00:06:18,541 --> 00:06:20,833
To me, my mom is
a key figure in this story.

138
00:06:20,916 --> 00:06:23,666
She's what we could call the driving
force behind the investigation.

139
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:24,791
MEMA VENTURINI'S GRANDSON

140
00:06:24,875 --> 00:06:28,583
She created the possibility
of bringing everything out in the open.

141
00:06:28,666 --> 00:06:30,958
I was working at the time,
and a neighbor called me,

142
00:06:31,041 --> 00:06:34,500
saying that my mom was unwell
and they had called an ambulance.

143
00:06:34,583 --> 00:06:37,333
What I felt at that moment,
you know, being so young

144
00:06:37,416 --> 00:06:39,291
and seeing all of it happen, was fear.

145
00:06:39,375 --> 00:06:40,541
MEMA VENTURINI'S GRANDDAUGHTER

146
00:06:40,625 --> 00:06:44,666
Because I kept thinking,
"My grandma is still a very young woman."

147
00:06:45,166 --> 00:06:46,708
"She's very strong."

148
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:51,166
That whole thing about her falling.
That she had fallen down the stairs.

149
00:06:51,250 --> 00:06:52,291
How did that happen?

150
00:06:53,083 --> 00:06:54,375
I think that was the first sign

151
00:06:54,458 --> 00:06:57,125
that something really odd
was actually happening.

152
00:06:57,208 --> 00:07:00,083
[Nélida] Come on, Mema, wake up! Come on!

153
00:07:00,166 --> 00:07:02,166
[mysterious music playing]

154
00:07:12,458 --> 00:07:13,833
[Horacio] And in that exact moment,

155
00:07:13,916 --> 00:07:16,333
a relative of the deceased
arrives at the scene.

156
00:07:16,416 --> 00:07:20,000
María de las Mercedes Bernardina
Bolla Ponte de Murano.

157
00:07:20,083 --> 00:07:21,083
"Yiya."

158
00:07:28,958 --> 00:07:30,708
Picture the scene.
Venturini's in the hallway…

159
00:07:30,791 --> 00:07:31,666
REPORTER

160
00:07:31,750 --> 00:07:34,083
…surrounded by neighbors
who don't know what's going on,

161
00:07:34,166 --> 00:07:36,208
and Yiya comes in carrying some pastries.

162
00:07:36,291 --> 00:07:38,083
A TALL, BLONDE, ELEGANT WOMAN

163
00:07:38,166 --> 00:07:40,000
SHE WAS CARRYING A PACKAGE OF PASTRIES

164
00:07:40,583 --> 00:07:44,041
She had a package of pastries
to eat with the deceased.

165
00:07:44,958 --> 00:07:47,166
Everyone knew her. She visited often.

166
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:49,166
She had been there the night before.

167
00:07:49,750 --> 00:07:52,458
As soon as she got there,
Yiya asked if she was alive,

168
00:07:52,541 --> 00:07:54,375
if she was going to survive.

169
00:07:54,458 --> 00:07:56,333
-If she had said anything.
-Did she say anything?

170
00:07:56,416 --> 00:07:57,416
Is she dead or alive?

171
00:07:58,166 --> 00:08:02,041
No, they were trying to bring her back.
She wasn't dead.

172
00:08:02,125 --> 00:08:03,333
-[Yiya] You have keys?
-Wake up!

173
00:08:03,416 --> 00:08:04,291
Keys to what?

174
00:08:04,375 --> 00:08:07,041
[Virginia] So Yiya says, "Okay, then.
Let's go into her apartment

175
00:08:07,125 --> 00:08:10,416
to check if there's a notebook with
her daughter Diana's phone number."

176
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:11,500
So they go.

177
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:15,500
[Horacio] But in the end,
she doesn't even call her,

178
00:08:15,583 --> 00:08:17,500
because she goes into the apartment,

179
00:08:17,583 --> 00:08:19,958
and instead of looking for
the notebook with the number,

180
00:08:20,041 --> 00:08:22,250
she makes sure to grab a piece of paper.

181
00:08:23,208 --> 00:08:24,625
[Yiya] Make sure to check.

182
00:08:25,125 --> 00:08:28,500
See if that notebook by the phone has
her daughter Diana's number in it.

183
00:08:28,583 --> 00:08:30,625
-[Mirtha] We welcome a special couple…
-Diana?

184
00:08:30,708 --> 00:08:33,083
-[Yiya] This is mine. Diana.
-[Mirtha] Please come in!

185
00:08:33,166 --> 00:08:34,666
-Welcome.
-Find anything? Under D?

186
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
So then, my mom says,
"How odd was what Yiya did?"

187
00:08:40,083 --> 00:08:42,166
"Going into the apartment.
What was she looking for?"

188
00:08:42,250 --> 00:08:43,833
"Oh, she came for the IOU slips."

189
00:08:43,916 --> 00:08:45,625
[door closes]

190
00:09:13,916 --> 00:09:16,250
That same night,
she went back to the police station

191
00:09:16,333 --> 00:09:17,875
to say that she was very angry,

192
00:09:17,958 --> 00:09:21,791
because her relative took advantage
of the fact that her mother was dying,

193
00:09:21,875 --> 00:09:26,625
and instead of worrying about her, went
to retrieve the IOU slip she'd left her,

194
00:09:26,708 --> 00:09:28,875
just so she wouldn't have to pay her debt.

195
00:09:28,958 --> 00:09:30,916
-Yiya. Her name's Yiya.
-[young Horacio] Last name?

196
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,791
Murano. Yiya Murano.

197
00:09:32,875 --> 00:09:35,083
-She owed my mom lots of money.
-[writing]

198
00:09:36,791 --> 00:09:41,333
Her daughter knew perfectly well that Yiya
owed her mother a large sum of money,

199
00:09:41,416 --> 00:09:44,083
and she was increasingly insisting
that Yiya repay her

200
00:09:44,166 --> 00:09:45,500
because she wanted to buy a house.

201
00:09:45,583 --> 00:09:49,500
Because of that new statement,
they order Yiya to be brought in.

202
00:09:49,583 --> 00:09:50,791
[door closes]

203
00:09:52,375 --> 00:09:55,375
-I don't understand why I'm here.
-[Horacio] How are you, ma'am?

204
00:09:56,083 --> 00:09:57,291
Good afternoon.

205
00:10:00,125 --> 00:10:01,291
[Horacio] What can you tell me

206
00:10:01,375 --> 00:10:04,791
about the death
of Zulema del Giorgio de Venturini?

207
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:07,000
My cousin Mema.

208
00:10:07,541 --> 00:10:09,208
-She had…
-[Horacio] We brought her in.

209
00:10:09,291 --> 00:10:10,875
We interrogated her about it.

210
00:10:10,958 --> 00:10:13,500
She denies it
and says she didn't take any slips.

211
00:10:13,583 --> 00:10:14,750
…very close, you know.

212
00:10:14,833 --> 00:10:17,541
I remember the times
we went to Mar del Plata…

213
00:10:17,625 --> 00:10:21,166
[Horacio] So we had nothing. We asked
the judge and he said to let her go.

214
00:10:21,250 --> 00:10:24,083
-I would win most of the time--
-[Horacio] Why go into the apartment?

215
00:10:26,083 --> 00:10:29,083
Would you be so kind as to give
me one of your cigarettes?

216
00:10:29,708 --> 00:10:31,708
[dramatic music playing]

217
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,791
But up to that point, people thought, "How
messed up that she's using her condition,

218
00:10:43,875 --> 00:10:47,250
or even her death,
as an excuse not to pay her back?"

219
00:10:47,833 --> 00:10:50,625
But the superintendent…
I believe it was him…

220
00:10:50,708 --> 00:10:52,833
says she took
a tiny bottle from the house.

221
00:10:52,916 --> 00:10:53,916
A DROPPER BOTTLE

222
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:57,083
Because Diana goes back
to her mother's building,

223
00:10:57,166 --> 00:10:59,166
and while she's talking
to the superintendent,

224
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:05,416
he tells her that Yiya took
a dropper bottle and put it in her purse.

225
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:07,250
-[Mirtha] We welcome a couple…
-This is mine.

226
00:11:07,333 --> 00:11:09,833
"THIS IS MINE"

227
00:11:09,916 --> 00:11:11,833
Your cousin just died.
Do you need to go inside

228
00:11:11,916 --> 00:11:14,708
and grab a dropper bottle
from the table because it's yours?

229
00:11:17,708 --> 00:11:19,583
[Horacio] Why do that? What was behind it?

230
00:11:19,666 --> 00:11:23,291
So instead of focusing on the scam,
I choose the dropper bottle.

231
00:11:23,375 --> 00:11:24,875
I go tell the judge what I'm thinking.

232
00:11:24,958 --> 00:11:28,375
"Look, sir, there's something off,
and the body's been handed over."

233
00:11:28,458 --> 00:11:30,791
But he tells me not to worry. Why?

234
00:11:30,875 --> 00:11:34,500
Because in these types of cases,
when the autopsy is performed,

235
00:11:34,583 --> 00:11:38,041
they make sure to preserve the stomach
and any viscera in formaldehyde…

236
00:11:38,125 --> 00:11:39,291
STOMACH CONTENTS

237
00:11:39,375 --> 00:11:41,375
…since it could be crucial
for any further analysis.

238
00:11:41,458 --> 00:11:44,583
That way, they are available
for any future investigation.

239
00:11:44,666 --> 00:11:46,291
THE PRESENCE OF TOXIC SUBSTANCES

240
00:11:47,291 --> 00:11:49,291
When he got the autopsy report,

241
00:11:49,375 --> 00:11:53,041
the judge concluded that she had
a quantity of cyanide in her body

242
00:11:53,125 --> 00:11:55,833
capable of wiping out a battalion.

243
00:11:55,916 --> 00:11:56,958
LETHAL DOSE

244
00:11:57,041 --> 00:11:58,416
POTASSIUM CYANIDE
SODIUM CYANIDE

245
00:11:58,500 --> 00:12:00,583
[Horacio] He realized
that a crime had been committed,

246
00:12:00,666 --> 00:12:03,708
enough to now initiate
a homicide investigation.

247
00:12:14,875 --> 00:12:16,791
[man] Women have taken part

248
00:12:16,875 --> 00:12:19,875
in many decisive moments
in the life of our country.

249
00:12:19,958 --> 00:12:21,041
5 WEEKS AFTER MEMA'S DEATH

250
00:12:21,125 --> 00:12:23,125
[younger Yiya] We scheduled
a trip to Mar del Plata.

251
00:12:23,208 --> 00:12:25,375
[man on TV] They had faith,
they believed in peace.

252
00:12:25,458 --> 00:12:27,291
But, yeah, everything, everything.

253
00:12:27,375 --> 00:12:29,416
Just think of all the fun
we would've had this summer.

254
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:32,791
[TV] They respected their convictions
and made sure others did too.

255
00:12:32,875 --> 00:12:35,375
-Eat it all.
-[Yiya] We thought we were going.

256
00:12:36,125 --> 00:12:38,750
-The country responded.
-[Yiya] We had booked the apartment.

257
00:12:38,833 --> 00:12:40,375
[doorbell rings]

258
00:12:42,791 --> 00:12:44,833
I remember it so vividly.

259
00:12:44,916 --> 00:12:48,458
Ignacia was going to get the door, and
my dad says, "No, no, I'll get the door."

260
00:12:48,541 --> 00:12:50,291
-Should I get that, sir?
-No, no.

261
00:12:50,375 --> 00:12:52,208
-[Yiya] It was…
-You expecting someone, Yiya?

262
00:12:52,291 --> 00:12:54,875
-[man on TV] It reaffirmed their faith.
-No, I'll go get it.

263
00:12:54,958 --> 00:12:57,541
-It gave them peace.
-[Yiya] …thinking about her…

264
00:12:57,625 --> 00:13:00,125
[Martín] "Sir, open the door.
Federal Police."

265
00:13:00,208 --> 00:13:01,083
[husband] Yes?

266
00:13:01,666 --> 00:13:03,708
They immediately handed him
a piece of paper,

267
00:13:03,791 --> 00:13:09,166
which I assume was some kind of warrant,
so they could come search the house.

268
00:13:09,250 --> 00:13:11,000
-[Yiya] To be honest, I…
-What's going on?

269
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:13,958
As my dad was reading it,
the officers just walked in

270
00:13:14,041 --> 00:13:16,166
and started looking
around the house for things.

271
00:13:16,250 --> 00:13:19,666
-[man on TV] The country defended…
-[Yiya] I'll call you later.

272
00:13:20,541 --> 00:13:22,166
[husband] What are you looking for?

273
00:13:22,791 --> 00:13:23,791
Just tell me.

274
00:13:23,875 --> 00:13:25,125
[man on TV] Today's woman

275
00:13:25,875 --> 00:13:29,916
knows she has earned her place as
a leading figure in present-day Argentina.

276
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:31,041
[husband] Sir.

277
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,291
Yiya got very nervous, and
they started searching the entire house.

278
00:13:35,375 --> 00:13:36,958
She followed them everywhere.

279
00:13:37,041 --> 00:13:39,583
-[man on TV] She also knows…
-[husband] What are you looking for?

280
00:13:40,166 --> 00:13:42,875
[Martín] "What could I have here?
Why are you opening my closet?"

281
00:13:42,958 --> 00:13:46,166
"Why are you searching our house?
What are you looking for?"

282
00:13:47,541 --> 00:13:49,291
[man on TV] That you reach that place

283
00:13:49,375 --> 00:13:54,250
with effort, integrity,
and a calling for greatness.

284
00:13:54,333 --> 00:13:55,916
That's my husband's medication.

285
00:13:56,791 --> 00:14:00,500
They seized all the medication
they ended up finding in the house,

286
00:14:00,583 --> 00:14:04,333
everything that might
have contained cyanide.

287
00:14:07,083 --> 00:14:12,125
Anything chemical, medications, pills,
in order to make a chemical comparison.

288
00:14:13,541 --> 00:14:15,958
Then they told my dad,
"The lady is coming with us."

289
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:18,666
[man on TV] That Argentine woman…

290
00:14:19,166 --> 00:14:20,791
-[Yiya] Can I grab my purse?
-[officer] No.

291
00:14:20,875 --> 00:14:22,375
-That leading lady…
-[officer] Let's go.

292
00:14:22,458 --> 00:14:24,666
[Martín] "Okay, let's go.
I have nothing to hide."

293
00:14:25,583 --> 00:14:26,625
[man on TV] …is you.

294
00:14:26,708 --> 00:14:28,208
[Yiya] It's just a mix-up.

295
00:14:29,250 --> 00:14:31,583
[Horacio] Yes, she was summoned
to the station as a detainee.

296
00:14:31,666 --> 00:14:32,916
[Yiya] Antonio, call my brother.

297
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,166
I'll call him. Don't you worry, Yiya.

298
00:14:35,250 --> 00:14:37,791
Everything will be fine.
We'll clear this up soon.

299
00:14:38,375 --> 00:14:41,958
She was put in a cell, and that was it.
And there she stayed.

300
00:14:42,041 --> 00:14:45,625
THUMBS - INDEX FINGERS - MIDDLE FINGERS -
RING FINGERS -PINKIES

301
00:14:45,708 --> 00:14:46,958
[camera shutter clicking]

302
00:14:47,583 --> 00:14:48,625
[rattling]

303
00:14:48,708 --> 00:14:49,833
[door slams]

304
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:53,291
Yiya's husband
just never believed for a second

305
00:14:53,375 --> 00:14:55,916
that she could ever be capable
of any such thing.

306
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,458
But he was extremely worried, and

307
00:14:59,208 --> 00:15:02,291
you could tell he was visibly upset
because of it all.

308
00:15:03,458 --> 00:15:05,625
The very next day,
he went to the police station

309
00:15:05,708 --> 00:15:07,500
and told me that
they wouldn't let him see her.

310
00:15:07,583 --> 00:15:09,000
She wasn't allowed to see anyone.

311
00:15:09,083 --> 00:15:10,333
KEEP IN PREVENTIVE CUSTODY

312
00:15:10,416 --> 00:15:13,208
[Virginia] She found herself prosecuted
by the criminal justice system

313
00:15:13,291 --> 00:15:15,583
at the height
of the military dictatorship.

314
00:15:16,583 --> 00:15:19,166
She was held in solitary
for five days at least,

315
00:15:19,250 --> 00:15:22,375
because at that time,
the Criminal Procedure Code allowed that.

316
00:15:22,458 --> 00:15:24,000
That would be unthinkable today.

317
00:15:24,083 --> 00:15:25,500
[Yiya] Hail Mary, full of grace…

318
00:15:25,583 --> 00:15:27,541
[Virginia] They'd leave them stewing,
so to speak.

319
00:15:27,625 --> 00:15:29,625
Blessed art thou among women,
and blessed is…

320
00:15:29,708 --> 00:15:33,291
You know, the police station
is a 100-year-old building.

321
00:15:33,375 --> 00:15:37,041
It's absolutely dismal.
It's the kind of place…

322
00:15:37,125 --> 00:15:40,333
The cells are just really horrible.

323
00:15:41,333 --> 00:15:43,416
I thought that would bring her down.

324
00:15:43,916 --> 00:15:46,333
-That it might loosen her up.
-Pray for us…

325
00:15:46,416 --> 00:15:50,458
And that it would make her
tell me things more directly.

326
00:15:50,541 --> 00:15:53,000
Because she'd be utterly broken.

327
00:15:53,083 --> 00:15:54,666
That would be the term we use.

328
00:15:54,750 --> 00:15:57,791
But you know, far from that.

329
00:15:57,875 --> 00:16:02,208
She was more exuberant
than when she was brought in.

330
00:16:02,291 --> 00:16:05,708
It seemed like she'd been
injected with joy.

331
00:16:05,791 --> 00:16:07,791
[pop ballad in Spanish playing]

332
00:16:17,916 --> 00:16:20,833
[Martín] If people were
to read the investigation files

333
00:16:20,916 --> 00:16:22,250
as they would a police case,

334
00:16:22,333 --> 00:16:24,333
they would very quickly understand

335
00:16:24,416 --> 00:16:27,833
what was behind
the actions Yiya had carried out.

336
00:16:27,916 --> 00:16:30,458
What they'd never understand
is her personality,

337
00:16:30,541 --> 00:16:32,875
what makes her Yiya Murano.

338
00:16:33,833 --> 00:16:36,875
[Horacio] Every detainee, no exception,
has to be seen by a medical examiner.

339
00:16:36,958 --> 00:16:37,791
EXPERT REPORT

340
00:16:37,875 --> 00:16:39,208
[Virginia] Psychiatric report.

341
00:16:39,291 --> 00:16:43,458
She says her name is María de las
Mercedes Bolla Aponte de Murano,

342
00:16:43,541 --> 00:16:45,500
and she's 48 years old.

343
00:16:46,625 --> 00:16:50,166
She began working
outside the home at the age of 19.

344
00:16:50,250 --> 00:16:53,833
In 1953, following her marriage,

345
00:16:53,916 --> 00:16:56,250
she decided to step down from the position

346
00:16:56,333 --> 00:17:00,291
to dedicate herself
to her new household responsibilities.

347
00:17:01,083 --> 00:17:04,250
She says that her husband,
a lawyer by profession,

348
00:17:04,333 --> 00:17:06,875
is a man of few words, very accommodating,

349
00:17:06,958 --> 00:17:10,458
and is someone entirely devoted
to his work and its daily demands.

350
00:17:10,541 --> 00:17:11,416
YIYA MURANO'S HUSBAND

351
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:17,000
Over time, this led to a real breakdown
in communication between the couple,

352
00:17:17,083 --> 00:17:20,625
leaving her with a profound sense
of loneliness and nostalgia.

353
00:17:20,708 --> 00:17:23,375
"VERY LONELY AND NOSTALGIC"

354
00:17:32,083 --> 00:17:34,500
[Horacio] She always claimed
that she was very religious.

355
00:17:34,583 --> 00:17:37,666
She kept mentioning
that she regularly went to Mass.

356
00:17:37,750 --> 00:17:40,208
[younger Yiya] Hail Mary,
full of grace, the Lord is with thee.

357
00:17:40,291 --> 00:17:43,916
[Horacio] To be honest, I think
that she also did it for show, you know?

358
00:17:44,833 --> 00:17:46,291
To keep up appearances.

359
00:17:49,125 --> 00:17:52,583
Yiya was my grandmother's cousin.
My grandma adored her,

360
00:17:52,666 --> 00:17:55,583
but I couldn't bring myself to like her.

361
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:04,791
To me, she seemed very manipulative.
She could be charming when it suited her,

362
00:18:04,875 --> 00:18:07,208
but otherwise, she'd be very rude.

363
00:18:07,291 --> 00:18:09,291
[car horns blaring]

364
00:18:10,791 --> 00:18:14,750
[Horacio] The fact is that Yiya was
someone who was extremely domineering.

365
00:18:14,833 --> 00:18:16,375
EXPANSIVE, OVERWHELMING

366
00:18:16,458 --> 00:18:17,458
VERY INSECURE, FEARFUL

367
00:18:17,541 --> 00:18:18,875
I mean, she needed to be liked…

368
00:18:18,958 --> 00:18:20,541
EGOCENTRIC AND NARCISSISTIC

369
00:18:20,625 --> 00:18:21,916
…and to be treated like a star.

370
00:18:25,791 --> 00:18:27,416
[Mariana] She was very elegant.

371
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:28,416
SHE LIKED TO DRESS WELL

372
00:18:28,500 --> 00:18:31,375
She wore clothes that you could
definitely tell looked expensive.

373
00:18:31,458 --> 00:18:32,833
SEDUCTIVE AND PSYCHOPATHIC BEHAVIOR

374
00:18:32,916 --> 00:18:34,541
She had a kind of magnetism about her…

375
00:18:34,625 --> 00:18:35,833
ALMOST THEATRICAL GESTURES

376
00:18:35,916 --> 00:18:37,666
…and acted like a queen
or some sort of diva.

377
00:18:38,833 --> 00:18:41,750
She was the kind of person
who acted as if

378
00:18:42,833 --> 00:18:44,041
other people were her subjects.

379
00:18:48,083 --> 00:18:53,541
Yiya convinced herself that
she actually belonged to high society.

380
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:58,625
Well, she would
put up a front that wasn't real.

381
00:18:58,708 --> 00:19:00,541
She wasn't fancy, she wasn't high-class.

382
00:19:04,041 --> 00:19:07,250
[Martín] She wasn't cultured.
Not in her speech, nor her knowledge,

383
00:19:07,333 --> 00:19:09,500
nor in the way she carried herself.

384
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:13,500
Even though her husband was a lawyer
and had his own firm,

385
00:19:13,583 --> 00:19:16,250
it seems more like
they were actually middle-class.

386
00:19:16,333 --> 00:19:18,333
[music continues on speaker]

387
00:19:20,125 --> 00:19:22,375
[Horacio] They only had
a one-bedroom apartment.

388
00:19:22,458 --> 00:19:26,333
In that same apartment
lived the couple, their son,

389
00:19:26,833 --> 00:19:28,791
and their live-in maid.

390
00:19:28,875 --> 00:19:30,250
They all lived there.

391
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:34,166
[music fades]

392
00:19:35,333 --> 00:19:37,791
[Martín] It was a simple building
in the Monserrat neighborhood.

393
00:19:37,875 --> 00:19:41,208
Across the street,
there were a lot of grocery stores,

394
00:19:41,291 --> 00:19:42,875
right next to each other.

395
00:19:44,166 --> 00:19:46,750
MONSERRAT
CITY OF BUENOS AIRES

396
00:19:46,833 --> 00:19:48,916
[Virginia] Monserrat is
a middle-class neighborhood.

397
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,583
It's a mix of things,
because it's downtown,

398
00:19:51,666 --> 00:19:53,291
but not in the financial district.

399
00:19:53,791 --> 00:19:57,500
It's a lively place during the week,
because of the many office buildings,

400
00:19:57,583 --> 00:20:02,375
and government buildings
like Congress, the Casa Rosada,

401
00:20:02,458 --> 00:20:04,583
and the Central Police Department.

402
00:20:04,666 --> 00:20:05,875
[siren blaring]

403
00:20:06,958 --> 00:20:09,750
[Virginia] Back in Yiya's time,
Avenida de Mayo,

404
00:20:09,833 --> 00:20:12,083
which runs through
the entire neighborhood,

405
00:20:12,166 --> 00:20:14,083
had an incredibly active social life.

406
00:20:14,166 --> 00:20:16,625
It was chock-full of cafés.

407
00:20:18,041 --> 00:20:20,750
It was also very close
to Avenida Corrientes,

408
00:20:20,833 --> 00:20:23,041
which is quite famous for its nightlife.

409
00:20:23,125 --> 00:20:26,000
There are movie theaters,
theaters, and restaurants.

410
00:20:26,083 --> 00:20:27,125
[jazz music playing]

411
00:20:31,208 --> 00:20:33,333
[Virginia] And that vibrant nightlife

412
00:20:33,833 --> 00:20:36,166
was the place where Yiya felt at home.

413
00:20:39,625 --> 00:20:42,208
Where she knew she could shine.

414
00:20:49,666 --> 00:20:52,375
6 WEEKS AFTER MEMA'S DEATH

415
00:20:57,875 --> 00:21:00,625
[Horacio] She was detained
and cut off from communication.

416
00:21:00,708 --> 00:21:02,000
This was standard procedure.

417
00:21:02,083 --> 00:21:05,458
But, you know, it's always customary
and considered protocol

418
00:21:05,541 --> 00:21:07,875
to make inquiries, to investigate.

419
00:21:08,041 --> 00:21:09,041
OFFICER HORACIO ROMEO

420
00:21:09,541 --> 00:21:13,208
[Horacio] I took advantage of the fact
that it was May 1st, a holiday.

421
00:21:13,291 --> 00:21:15,666
I expected people would be at their homes,

422
00:21:15,750 --> 00:21:18,000
so I went over to her building
to investigate.

423
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:21,708
MÉXICO 1177

424
00:21:23,416 --> 00:21:27,458
At that time, it was still customary
to have signs over the door,

425
00:21:27,541 --> 00:21:30,041
and I noticed that
one of them was for a doctor.

426
00:21:30,791 --> 00:21:32,875
-I rang the bell.
-[doorbell buzzing]

427
00:21:32,958 --> 00:21:36,125
[Horacio] And, luckily for me,
he knew Mrs. Murano very well,

428
00:21:36,625 --> 00:21:39,500
because his wife happened
to be part of a group of friends

429
00:21:39,583 --> 00:21:42,583
to whom Yiya had approached with

430
00:21:43,791 --> 00:21:45,666
investment proposals

431
00:21:45,750 --> 00:21:48,083
that offered very high interest.

432
00:21:48,166 --> 00:21:51,333
He said he knew that her friends
had invested large sums,

433
00:21:51,416 --> 00:21:54,041
but he didn't like it.

434
00:21:54,625 --> 00:21:58,291
Because of that, he didn't
allow his wife to invest any money.

435
00:21:58,375 --> 00:22:01,458
And at one point in the conversation,
he says to me,

436
00:22:01,541 --> 00:22:04,541
"Poor woman, though,
she had such bad luck,

437
00:22:04,625 --> 00:22:08,291
because those ladies just kept dying."

438
00:22:08,458 --> 00:22:10,416
HE RECALLED TWO OTHER FRIENDS OF HERS

439
00:22:10,500 --> 00:22:12,416
HAD CEASED TO EXIST

440
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:14,541
UNDER UNCLEAR CIRCUMSTANCES

441
00:22:14,625 --> 00:22:17,541
He said, "First it was Nilda,

442
00:22:17,625 --> 00:22:21,708
then it was Ayala,
and now Venturini, as well."

443
00:22:22,208 --> 00:22:25,083
Ah! I got goosebumps everywhere!

444
00:22:25,166 --> 00:22:28,375
I completely lost it!
What? I couldn't believe it.

445
00:22:28,458 --> 00:22:30,541
I was anxious
to get out of there immediately

446
00:22:30,625 --> 00:22:32,958
and go look for all these people.

447
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:36,500
[suspenseful music playing]

448
00:22:41,250 --> 00:22:43,250
[Horacio] First it was Mrs. Ayala.

449
00:22:43,333 --> 00:22:46,666
She had a son who was a cadet
at the Naval Prefecture school.

450
00:22:46,750 --> 00:22:47,958
LELIA FORMISANO DE AYALA'S SON

451
00:22:48,041 --> 00:22:50,583
In that year,
I remember I was studying in Zárate,

452
00:22:50,666 --> 00:22:53,541
at the Argentine Naval Prefecture school.

453
00:22:54,041 --> 00:23:00,250
Then, during my second year of studies at
that school, well, my mother passed away.

454
00:23:01,500 --> 00:23:04,416
She died in the apartment
on Avenida Belgrano.

455
00:23:06,583 --> 00:23:08,291
[muffled voices inside]

456
00:23:08,375 --> 00:23:10,541
4 WEEKS BEFORE MEMA'S DEATH

457
00:23:10,625 --> 00:23:13,625
[Eloy] How did they find out?
Because of the smell of decomposition.

458
00:23:13,708 --> 00:23:15,500
SHE WASN'T ANSWERING THE INTERCOM

459
00:23:15,583 --> 00:23:17,375
THE TV WAS ON

460
00:23:17,458 --> 00:23:19,041
A DISTINCT SMELL

461
00:23:19,125 --> 00:23:20,625
FROM A CORPSE

462
00:23:20,708 --> 00:23:23,416
[man on TV] Well, I hear you,
but there's nothing I can do for you now.

463
00:23:23,500 --> 00:23:24,916
[Eloy] The police intervened.

464
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:28,208
They entered through
an adjoining apartment that was connected,

465
00:23:28,291 --> 00:23:29,875
and found her dead.

466
00:23:29,958 --> 00:23:34,375
Sitting on the table next to her
were pastries.

467
00:23:34,458 --> 00:23:36,708
THERE WAS A TRAY,
LIKE FROM A BAKERY, WITH PASTRIES

468
00:23:36,791 --> 00:23:39,500
[Virginia] There were leftovers.
From what we know, they were éclairs.

469
00:23:39,583 --> 00:23:40,833
SOMEONE RECALLS SEEING AN ÉCLAIR

470
00:23:40,916 --> 00:23:45,625
It's sort of like…
a pastry filled with custard or cream.

471
00:23:47,250 --> 00:23:50,083
Yiya had been having tea there,
and she left.

472
00:23:50,166 --> 00:23:54,750
THE LAST TIME SHE WAS SEEN ALIVE,
SHE WAS WITH THE DEFENDANT

473
00:23:55,791 --> 00:23:59,291
A funeral home doctor had been
involved in signing a death certificate

474
00:23:59,375 --> 00:24:02,458
for myocardial infarction,
which was standard practice.

475
00:24:03,458 --> 00:24:06,958
It seemed her death had been… natural.

476
00:24:07,458 --> 00:24:10,333
We held the funeral and
I had two or three days off,

477
00:24:10,416 --> 00:24:12,500
then I went straight back to school.

478
00:24:13,416 --> 00:24:16,625
I called the cadet school.
They brought him to the phone.

479
00:24:17,125 --> 00:24:19,458
And I asked him the usual questions.

480
00:24:19,958 --> 00:24:23,916
"Did anyone owe your mother money?"
"Yes, Yiya Murano owed her money."

481
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,041
"And did she ask her to pay her back?"

482
00:24:26,125 --> 00:24:28,166
"Yes, my mom wanted to go to Europe,

483
00:24:28,250 --> 00:24:31,375
and she had asked Yiya
to give her back the money."

484
00:24:31,458 --> 00:24:33,583
"And she died." [chuckles]

485
00:24:33,666 --> 00:24:35,666
[light, mysterious music playing]

486
00:24:39,291 --> 00:24:42,833
[Horacio] Nilda Gamba, one of
the other investors, was her neighbor.

487
00:24:42,916 --> 00:24:44,291
Yiya's neighbor.

488
00:24:45,375 --> 00:24:49,458
I'm Jorge Arona,
I'm the nephew of Nilda Gamba.

489
00:24:49,541 --> 00:24:53,833
I was there with her
in the very last moments of her life,

490
00:24:53,916 --> 00:24:56,666
because I lived very close by.

491
00:24:56,750 --> 00:24:58,875
THIRD ANNIVERSARY
OF THE NATIONAL REORGANIZATION PROCESS

492
00:24:58,958 --> 00:25:01,916
[man] This is what was happening
before March 24th, 1976.

493
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:03,291
6 WEEKS BEFORE MEMA'S DEATH

494
00:25:03,375 --> 00:25:06,250
Disorder, speculation, terrorism.

495
00:25:07,125 --> 00:25:09,333
[Arona] They called me
because she was feeling unwell.

496
00:25:09,416 --> 00:25:12,458
When I got there, I approached her.
The doctor was treating her.

497
00:25:12,541 --> 00:25:15,500
[man on TV] You've lived it.
Remember and compare.

498
00:25:15,583 --> 00:25:17,458
MISS GAMBA WAS FEELING UNWELL

499
00:25:18,041 --> 00:25:19,000
SHE WAS IN PAIN

500
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:20,916
SEVERE NAUSEA

501
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:25,750
Someone else was there.
It might've been Yiya, I can't remember.

502
00:25:26,583 --> 00:25:28,541
They lived super close, door to door.

503
00:25:28,625 --> 00:25:32,833
That explains why,
when Nilda started feeling sick,

504
00:25:32,916 --> 00:25:37,291
her friend Yiya was the first person
they called to take care of her.

505
00:25:37,791 --> 00:25:42,125
What the investigation file reports is
that Nilda experienced indigestion

506
00:25:42,208 --> 00:25:43,375
after having eaten fish.

507
00:25:43,458 --> 00:25:45,000
[Nilda] I ate tuna with spaghetti.

508
00:25:45,083 --> 00:25:47,583
[Virginia] She apparently had
some digestive issues,

509
00:25:47,666 --> 00:25:49,916
and her friend Yiya would
come give her herbal teas.

510
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:53,458
I brought you some digestive tea.
Drink it, and you'll feel much better.

511
00:25:53,541 --> 00:25:56,375
It was like a series of ups and downs.

512
00:25:56,458 --> 00:25:59,250
She would feel much better,
then feel much worse.

513
00:25:59,333 --> 00:26:00,750
Yiya was there, taking care of her.

514
00:26:00,833 --> 00:26:03,291
ACCOMPANIED BY HER NEIGHBOR,
MRS. BOLLA DE MURANO

515
00:26:03,375 --> 00:26:05,125
[Nilda gasping]

516
00:26:05,208 --> 00:26:06,208
[Virginia] And then…

517
00:26:06,291 --> 00:26:07,291
[Nilda sighs]

518
00:26:07,375 --> 00:26:08,458
[Virginia] …she died.

519
00:26:10,791 --> 00:26:13,708
At that time,
nobody had any theories about it.

520
00:26:13,791 --> 00:26:16,166
They assumed
that she had a glucose spike

521
00:26:16,250 --> 00:26:19,833
or some problem
related to her actual diabetes.

522
00:26:21,458 --> 00:26:23,333
And the circumstances were the same.

523
00:26:23,416 --> 00:26:25,250
Yiya owed her money,

524
00:26:25,333 --> 00:26:27,541
she did not get her money back,

525
00:26:27,625 --> 00:26:29,958
and she died. [chuckles]

526
00:26:30,041 --> 00:26:32,041
[dramatic music playing]

527
00:26:34,125 --> 00:26:36,833
[Horacio] Then I immediately put
all the pieces together.

528
00:26:36,916 --> 00:26:39,333
But you don't always
see the whole picture.

529
00:26:39,416 --> 00:26:42,500
So you want to make sure
you give the judge the information

530
00:26:42,583 --> 00:26:44,416
and see what he decides.

531
00:26:46,208 --> 00:26:49,416
He was calm, told me to relax,
like he did before.

532
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:51,791
"Relax," because I was too excited.

533
00:26:51,875 --> 00:26:55,500
But this time, he said, "Okay,
we're going to have the bodies exhumed."

534
00:26:56,083 --> 00:26:59,583
"I'll go ahead and give the order,
and we'll see what happens."

535
00:27:03,208 --> 00:27:04,875
[Virginia] Both deaths
were deemed natural.

536
00:27:04,958 --> 00:27:07,125
Their homes had been cleaned,
things thrown out.

537
00:27:07,208 --> 00:27:09,041
They could only piece together
what had happened

538
00:27:09,125 --> 00:27:13,500
by conducting a chemical analysis
of the two women, who were already buried.

539
00:27:20,166 --> 00:27:22,083
[commentator] Unbelievable!

540
00:27:24,083 --> 00:27:28,708
Yiya was in jail then, and the neighbors
started spreading around rumors.

541
00:27:29,750 --> 00:27:31,833
They said my dad had been cheated on,

542
00:27:31,916 --> 00:27:34,125
that she left him
and ran away with a lover.

543
00:27:35,416 --> 00:27:37,166
A little while later,

544
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:39,708
the doorbell rang
at nine o'clock at night.

545
00:27:39,791 --> 00:27:41,791
-[doorbell rings]
-[Ignacia] Should I get that, sir?

546
00:27:41,875 --> 00:27:43,416
[Antonio] No, no, no. I'll go get it.

547
00:27:44,458 --> 00:27:46,958
Ugh, who could it be at this time?

548
00:27:48,875 --> 00:27:53,083
[Martín] When the reporters arrived,
my dad opened the door.

549
00:27:53,166 --> 00:27:54,833
-[Antonio] Yes?
-[man] Good evening.

550
00:27:54,916 --> 00:27:57,375
What can you tell us
about the accusations against your wife?

551
00:27:57,458 --> 00:28:01,500
[Martín] It was naive of him to do so,
but he had no idea why they were there.

552
00:28:01,583 --> 00:28:03,416
[Antonio] Don't take
pictures of me in my house!

553
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:05,750
[Martín] When he began listening to them,

554
00:28:05,833 --> 00:28:08,541
his expression and demeanor
began changing,

555
00:28:08,625 --> 00:28:13,000
which is completely understandable,
because he really had no idea.

556
00:28:13,583 --> 00:28:15,250
And then they began telling us…

557
00:28:15,333 --> 00:28:16,958
She poisoned her friends.

558
00:28:17,041 --> 00:28:20,583
[Martín] …what, to me, seemed like
a very farfetched story.

559
00:28:20,666 --> 00:28:21,791
[Antonio] …all the time…

560
00:28:21,875 --> 00:28:26,666
[Martín] "There are three women
to whom Mrs. Murano owed money."

561
00:28:26,750 --> 00:28:27,916
[Antonio] This makes no sense.

562
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,416
-"The three of them were found dead."
-[Antonio] Unbelievable.

563
00:28:30,500 --> 00:28:34,291
"And the last person to see them alive,
all three of them,

564
00:28:35,333 --> 00:28:36,666
was your wife."

565
00:28:37,250 --> 00:28:38,833
THE POISONER
SHOCKING REVELATIONS

566
00:28:38,916 --> 00:28:40,583
THE POISONER'S STORY

567
00:28:40,666 --> 00:28:42,333
THE YIYA CASE

568
00:28:42,416 --> 00:28:43,958
[dramatic music playing]

569
00:28:44,041 --> 00:28:45,708
A FRIEND TO DEATH!

570
00:28:45,791 --> 00:28:46,875
MURDERER AND SCAMMER

571
00:28:49,625 --> 00:28:51,583
[Martín] At that time,
the reporters had nothing.

572
00:28:51,666 --> 00:28:52,708
TEA, FRIENDS AND A MYSTERY

573
00:28:52,791 --> 00:28:55,958
Since they didn't have a single piece of
information from those of us close to her…

574
00:28:56,041 --> 00:28:57,625
THERE MAY BE MORE

575
00:28:57,708 --> 00:28:58,958
…they'd make up their own story.

576
00:28:59,041 --> 00:29:00,208
NOW SEVEN VICTIMS
NOW EIGHT!

577
00:29:00,291 --> 00:29:02,583
THE POISONER:
TEN HEARSES HIRED

578
00:29:03,083 --> 00:29:04,500
THREE? FIVE? TEN? NONE? HOW MANY?

579
00:29:04,583 --> 00:29:08,083
[Martín] One of them would make something
up, and another one would copy it.

580
00:29:08,583 --> 00:29:11,333
Then a third one would change
just a couple of words, and so on.

581
00:29:11,416 --> 00:29:13,958
DIABOLICAL
WELL-TO-DO AND DIABOLICAL

582
00:29:15,291 --> 00:29:17,291
However you look at it, it's a good story.

583
00:29:17,375 --> 00:29:20,541
And she's not the quiet,
discreet type. She's a dynamo!

584
00:29:21,666 --> 00:29:22,666
[Horacio] And it blew up.

585
00:29:22,750 --> 00:29:25,083
And reporters were constantly
knocking on my door.

586
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:27,625
YIYA'S HOUSE
YIYA'S TABLE

587
00:29:28,625 --> 00:29:30,416
A TORNADO OF A WOMAN

588
00:29:30,500 --> 00:29:31,583
HOW DIABOLICAL KILLER LIVES

589
00:29:31,666 --> 00:29:33,625
[Martín] And it turned into
an unstoppable snowball

590
00:29:33,708 --> 00:29:35,333
that ended with accusations of witchcraft.

591
00:29:35,416 --> 00:29:37,125
SHE PRACTICED "BLACK MAGIC"
SHE HAD 2 LOVERS

592
00:29:37,208 --> 00:29:40,666
[Martín] They claimed Yiya held gatherings
where they practiced black magic.

593
00:29:40,750 --> 00:29:42,333
POSSESSED BY "BLACK MAGIC"

594
00:29:42,416 --> 00:29:43,583
LOVER OF STRANGE RITUALS

595
00:29:43,666 --> 00:29:47,416
The whole thing began feeling
like it was straight out of a movie.

596
00:29:47,500 --> 00:29:48,541
[chuckles]

597
00:29:48,625 --> 00:29:50,583
ENIGMAS SURROUND THE YIYA CASE

598
00:29:50,666 --> 00:29:52,833
[Martín] The method
of administering the poison…

599
00:29:52,916 --> 00:29:53,875
CYANIDE: THE ENIGMA

600
00:29:53,958 --> 00:29:56,875
…varied depending
on the source reporting it.

601
00:29:56,958 --> 00:29:57,916
CYANIDE AT TEA TIME

602
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,250
[Martín] The poison was in the tea,
it was in the pastries.

603
00:30:01,208 --> 00:30:03,708
It's common practice worldwide,
no matter where…

604
00:30:03,791 --> 00:30:05,416
POISONER

605
00:30:05,500 --> 00:30:08,083
[Martín] …for a serial killer
to be given a name.

606
00:30:08,166 --> 00:30:11,416
And Yiya was The Poisoner of Monserrat.

607
00:30:11,500 --> 00:30:15,958
THE POISONER OF MONSERRAT

608
00:30:16,541 --> 00:30:18,000
[woman] Police Commander,

609
00:30:18,083 --> 00:30:20,750
can you share your opinion
on the Murano case?

610
00:30:20,833 --> 00:30:23,791
I'd say it's your typical female homicide.

611
00:30:24,458 --> 00:30:27,583
Because, due to her physical capacities,

612
00:30:27,666 --> 00:30:30,625
a woman cannot commit a violent homicide,

613
00:30:30,708 --> 00:30:33,875
if she's actually inclined
to even commit murder.

614
00:30:33,958 --> 00:30:37,708
And so she resorts to this, to poisoning.

615
00:30:37,791 --> 00:30:41,083
SODIUM CYANIDE

616
00:30:42,958 --> 00:30:45,666
Poison is a substance capable
of harming any living being.

617
00:30:45,750 --> 00:30:46,666
TOXICOLOGICAL EXPERT

618
00:30:46,750 --> 00:30:49,750
Cyanide and carbon monoxide,
in particular, are silent poisons,

619
00:30:49,833 --> 00:30:52,916
and because of that,
have long been favored by perpetrators

620
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,708
as their weapon of choice, so to speak,

621
00:30:55,791 --> 00:30:59,500
because the victim often falls into a trap
and consumes them unknowingly,

622
00:30:59,583 --> 00:31:00,708
either in a drink…

623
00:31:00,791 --> 00:31:02,083
CYANIDE
KING OF TOXIC SUBSTANCES

624
00:31:02,166 --> 00:31:03,500
…or, even more commonly, in food.

625
00:31:03,583 --> 00:31:05,375
When it comes to cyanide, though,

626
00:31:05,458 --> 00:31:07,958
we consistently had
cases of cyanide poisoning,

627
00:31:08,041 --> 00:31:10,958
but those cases were mostly
poisoning due to suicide.

628
00:31:11,958 --> 00:31:15,125
I know that, during moments of subversion,

629
00:31:15,208 --> 00:31:19,041
extremists would carry
a small cyanide capsule

630
00:31:19,125 --> 00:31:21,541
so they had
the option to commit suicide.

631
00:31:21,625 --> 00:31:23,125
That was common knowledge.

632
00:31:24,791 --> 00:31:27,875
There were also criminal cases,
but those were always cases

633
00:31:27,958 --> 00:31:29,708
where the poison was intentionally used

634
00:31:29,791 --> 00:31:33,791
in doses that caused the victims'
sudden and immediate death.

635
00:31:33,875 --> 00:31:37,375
The big problem
in Yiya Murano's case was the cyanide,

636
00:31:37,458 --> 00:31:41,125
which is a fast-acting poison, but
it hadn't killed her victims instantly.

637
00:31:41,208 --> 00:31:45,125
I mean, there was, sort of,
delay in each one of their deaths.

638
00:31:45,208 --> 00:31:47,666
It was the exact dose to kill them,

639
00:31:48,166 --> 00:31:51,291
but not enough
to poison any of them immediately.

640
00:31:52,375 --> 00:31:54,208
It was completely unprecedented.

641
00:31:54,291 --> 00:31:57,500
It was the first time
that something like that ever happened.

642
00:31:59,458 --> 00:32:02,791
[woman] This is the entrance
for 1177 Mexico,

643
00:32:02,875 --> 00:32:05,666
which is the building that,
for the last 22 years,

644
00:32:05,750 --> 00:32:09,458
has been the home to Mrs. María
de las Mercedes Bolla Ponte de Murano.

645
00:32:09,541 --> 00:32:14,333
And here is the restaurant La María,
where she would often come for dinner.

646
00:32:14,416 --> 00:32:16,083
What was her favorite food?

647
00:32:16,583 --> 00:32:19,750
Well, her boy always wanted milanesa,

648
00:32:20,250 --> 00:32:23,750
french fries or even fried egg,
but she always told him no.

649
00:32:24,333 --> 00:32:26,375
She wanted him to eat steak and salad.

650
00:32:26,458 --> 00:32:27,875
[woman] Did she ever say why?

651
00:32:27,958 --> 00:32:30,583
Well, yeah.
She didn't want him gaining weight.

652
00:32:32,833 --> 00:32:35,375
[Martín] You know, for me,
it always seemed very strange

653
00:32:35,458 --> 00:32:38,291
to see mothers going
to pick up their kids from school,

654
00:32:38,375 --> 00:32:42,125
and to see them hugging,
and then their kids running to them,

655
00:32:42,208 --> 00:32:45,083
and their moms would
carry their backpacks.

656
00:32:46,500 --> 00:32:47,833
I never had that.

657
00:32:50,291 --> 00:32:53,083
[Mariana] Martín was my age
and we got along well,

658
00:32:53,166 --> 00:32:57,333
but it was quite unsettling to witness
Martín's relationship with his mom.

659
00:32:57,416 --> 00:32:59,125
She treated him poorly.

660
00:32:59,208 --> 00:33:02,125
She looked down on him.
Treated him like he was beneath her.

661
00:33:02,208 --> 00:33:06,041
Like he wasn't smart,
or like he just wasn't capable.

662
00:33:07,291 --> 00:33:08,750
[young Martín] Mom, no.

663
00:33:08,833 --> 00:33:13,375
[Martín] Yiya used to take me to the café
of a famous hotel on Avenida de Mayo.

664
00:33:15,041 --> 00:33:16,958
Don't eat like an animal, Martín.
Sit up straight.

665
00:33:17,041 --> 00:33:18,791
Can't you see people are looking?

666
00:33:20,500 --> 00:33:26,000
[Martín] To me, it was quite normal
that a woman would go out every morning

667
00:33:26,083 --> 00:33:29,833
to have breakfast
with some family friend, alone.

668
00:33:29,916 --> 00:33:31,375
[Yiya] How are you? [laughs]

669
00:33:31,458 --> 00:33:32,333
[kisses]

670
00:33:32,416 --> 00:33:35,666
[Martín] Keep in mind
that being nine back then

671
00:33:35,750 --> 00:33:39,041
was not the same as being nine today.

672
00:33:40,708 --> 00:33:43,083
-I had no idea what a lover was.
-[Yiya] All here?

673
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:45,916
[man] I'll bring you
what you asked for tomorrow.

674
00:33:46,416 --> 00:33:47,875
[Martín] I had to keep this a secret.

675
00:33:47,958 --> 00:33:51,958
And anyway, I couldn't have told my dad,
because they spoke in code.

676
00:33:52,041 --> 00:33:55,166
[man] What are we having tonight?
How about dessert?

677
00:33:55,250 --> 00:33:58,041
-[Yiya] Well, yes, we must have dessert.
-[man] Yes.

678
00:33:58,125 --> 00:33:59,333
[Martín] And one of her lovers…

679
00:33:59,416 --> 00:34:00,875
-[Yiya] Or it's not fun.
-[man] Right.

680
00:34:00,958 --> 00:34:04,208
[Martín] …when I told him something,
like "Oh, yeah, my dad said…"

681
00:34:04,291 --> 00:34:07,708
"No," he said.
"Call him Antonio. I'm your dad."

682
00:34:09,416 --> 00:34:10,666
WAITER AT CASTELAR HOTEL

683
00:34:10,750 --> 00:34:13,833
I knew her,
because she was extremely elegant,

684
00:34:13,916 --> 00:34:16,041
so she attracted lots of attention.

685
00:34:16,125 --> 00:34:18,250
And one day,
one of the guys who waited on her

686
00:34:18,333 --> 00:34:21,208
came up and said,
"Look who's sitting over there."

687
00:34:21,875 --> 00:34:24,250
So, everyone looked
and asked, "Wait, where?"

688
00:34:24,333 --> 00:34:27,375
"That lady over there.
That's Juanita Martínez."

689
00:34:27,458 --> 00:34:28,666
ARGENTINE COMIC THEATER COMPANY

690
00:34:28,750 --> 00:34:31,875
[Martín] Many years ago,
there was a comedian named José Marrone,

691
00:34:31,958 --> 00:34:34,625
and he was married to Juanita Martínez,
who was an actress,

692
00:34:34,708 --> 00:34:36,208
and she looked a lot like Yiya.

693
00:34:36,291 --> 00:34:39,541
And Yiya would even sign autographs
pretending to be her.

694
00:34:40,791 --> 00:34:43,666
[Juan] She was not Juanita Martínez,
but she looked a lot like her.

695
00:34:43,750 --> 00:34:45,708
She was a regular, for sure.

696
00:34:45,791 --> 00:34:49,333
She'd come in the morning
and she'd have her morning tea.

697
00:34:49,416 --> 00:34:53,583
She loved all the cream-filled pastries,
and the puff pastries.

698
00:34:53,666 --> 00:34:56,333
And she loved the dulce de leche cannolis.

699
00:34:56,416 --> 00:34:57,833
She absolutely had a sweet tooth.

700
00:35:01,541 --> 00:35:03,500
This was some time
before everything happened.

701
00:35:03,583 --> 00:35:05,333
A year or two earlier, I can't remember.

702
00:35:05,416 --> 00:35:08,958
We stopped at a café, and a man showed up.

703
00:35:09,041 --> 00:35:11,333
-I believe he was called Héctor.
-[Yiya] How are you?

704
00:35:11,416 --> 00:35:14,541
[Mariana] I think I remember him
as being a bald man.

705
00:35:14,625 --> 00:35:16,958
-What have we got here?
-[Mariana] And we had breakfast.

706
00:35:17,041 --> 00:35:19,208
-Yiya, Héctor…
-It's all here?

707
00:35:19,291 --> 00:35:20,791
-[Mariana] …and me.
-[Héctor] Of course.

708
00:35:21,541 --> 00:35:23,750
When we left, Yiya told me,

709
00:35:23,833 --> 00:35:27,250
"Don't say a word about what happened."

710
00:35:29,291 --> 00:35:32,375
Juanita used to call the man
who was with her "the doctor."

711
00:35:32,458 --> 00:35:34,208
She said he was her husband.

712
00:35:36,125 --> 00:35:39,875
[Virginia] Héctor Cantón was a doctor
who was Yiya's most consistent lover.

713
00:35:39,958 --> 00:35:42,791
He testified that he didn't know
if her husband knew

714
00:35:42,875 --> 00:35:45,500
about his relationship with his wife,

715
00:35:46,750 --> 00:35:49,750
but that he was totally integrated
into Yiya's circle.

716
00:35:49,833 --> 00:35:54,291
She managed to make
those two things successfully coexist.

717
00:35:56,458 --> 00:35:58,458
[Juan] And then later,
when everything happened,

718
00:35:58,541 --> 00:36:00,750
I saw a picture of her with her husband.

719
00:36:00,833 --> 00:36:02,916
And the doctor was not her husband.

720
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:06,291
That's when I thought,
"Well, something's off." [chuckles]

721
00:36:07,583 --> 00:36:08,750
Then she stopped coming

722
00:36:09,708 --> 00:36:12,916
and we found out later
from a reporter, I remember.

723
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:17,333
He said, "You know who's in jail?"
"No." "Juanita," he said.

724
00:36:18,750 --> 00:36:19,875
[door closes]

725
00:36:20,958 --> 00:36:24,583
[Horacio] I had her brought in
so then I could take her statement.

726
00:36:26,375 --> 00:36:30,083
At one point, she decided she wanted
to actually tell us what she'd done.

727
00:36:30,166 --> 00:36:31,458
I'm going to tell you everything.

728
00:36:31,541 --> 00:36:33,458
[Horacio] That was called
a spontaneous statement.

729
00:36:33,541 --> 00:36:34,750
EXPRESSES HER DESIRE TO EXPLAIN

730
00:36:35,416 --> 00:36:37,750
[Horacio] Our medical examiner
had said to me,

731
00:36:37,833 --> 00:36:41,416
"You take her statement
for half an hour or 15 minutes, tops."

732
00:36:41,500 --> 00:36:43,708
"Then you leave,
go have a coffee, and come back."

733
00:36:43,791 --> 00:36:45,458
And I asked him, "Why?"

734
00:36:46,166 --> 00:36:48,083
I was a young officer then,

735
00:36:49,625 --> 00:36:50,750
full of energy.

736
00:36:51,250 --> 00:36:53,166
I was used to dealing with criminals,

737
00:36:53,250 --> 00:36:56,083
I thought, "That's…"
I didn't listen to him.

738
00:36:56,166 --> 00:36:57,583
Some time ago,

739
00:36:58,083 --> 00:37:02,583
I had to undergo a surgical intervention
due to a condition I was suffering from.

740
00:37:02,666 --> 00:37:05,125
[Horacio] And I continued
taking her statement.

741
00:37:05,208 --> 00:37:06,958
SURGERY FOR HEMORRHOIDS UNSUCCESSFUL

742
00:37:07,041 --> 00:37:09,416
[Horacio] The doctor was right.

743
00:37:09,500 --> 00:37:11,666
She steered the entire conversation.

744
00:37:11,750 --> 00:37:14,208
What truly matters to me
are my loved ones, of course.

745
00:37:14,291 --> 00:37:16,541
That's why I vowed to Our Lady of Lourdes,

746
00:37:16,625 --> 00:37:19,541
to whom I've been profoundly
devoted for all my life,

747
00:37:19,625 --> 00:37:22,083
that I would renounce
every one of my worldly possessions.

748
00:37:22,166 --> 00:37:23,958
SHE WOULDN'T WEAR ANY OTHER JEWELRY

749
00:37:24,666 --> 00:37:26,791
[Yiya] I decided that I would give them

750
00:37:26,875 --> 00:37:30,125
to all of my dearest friends
and my loved ones.

751
00:37:30,750 --> 00:37:31,708
GAVE AWAY PERSONAL ITEMS

752
00:37:31,791 --> 00:37:34,125
[Horacio] She was manipulative,
managed timing her perfectly…

753
00:37:34,208 --> 00:37:35,583
OTTER-SKIN COAT
BLACK FAUX-FUR COAT

754
00:37:35,666 --> 00:37:37,166
…and controlled people very skillfully.

755
00:37:37,250 --> 00:37:39,416
[Yiya] Because to me,
what really matters the most…

756
00:37:39,500 --> 00:37:42,000
[Horacio] She managed
to steer the conversation

757
00:37:42,083 --> 00:37:44,041
whichever way she wanted to.

758
00:37:44,125 --> 00:37:45,125
…and is always with me.

759
00:37:45,208 --> 00:37:47,250
That's when I remembered
the advice and said, "Wait!"

760
00:37:48,875 --> 00:37:52,750
I left for coffee, came back, and
said to her, "All right, where were we?"

761
00:37:52,833 --> 00:37:58,583
So that way, I'd interrupt her…
control of the narrative.

762
00:38:01,291 --> 00:38:03,750
[Virginia] The only time
that she seemed a bit nervous

763
00:38:03,833 --> 00:38:08,375
was when they began asking her
over and over and over again

764
00:38:09,125 --> 00:38:11,166
where she'd get the money
to pay them back.

765
00:38:11,250 --> 00:38:13,166
That's when her façade cracked.

766
00:38:13,250 --> 00:38:17,375
And she then said, "From my friends.
I've told you, like, 40 times."

767
00:38:17,458 --> 00:38:18,666
FROM "CERTAIN FRIENDS"

768
00:38:18,750 --> 00:38:20,166
FROM "MALE FRIENDS"

769
00:38:20,250 --> 00:38:21,791
"WELL-TO-DO GENTLEMEN"

770
00:38:21,875 --> 00:38:23,083
[man] There we go.

771
00:38:27,458 --> 00:38:30,208
[Horacio] Because she said her lovers
supported her financially.

772
00:38:30,291 --> 00:38:31,458
400 OR 500 DOLLARS EACH TIME

773
00:38:31,541 --> 00:38:33,916
And if she ever asked
them for money to pay something back,

774
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:35,208
they'd lend it to her.

775
00:38:35,958 --> 00:38:39,958
Her lovers later testified
that they had indeed paid for her coffee

776
00:38:40,041 --> 00:38:42,458
and occasionally gave her some money,

777
00:38:42,541 --> 00:38:45,458
but it was never a big enough sum
for her to live on.

778
00:38:47,625 --> 00:38:50,375
But she wanted to use that to justify

779
00:38:50,458 --> 00:38:52,375
how she sustained the lifestyle she had.

780
00:38:52,458 --> 00:38:53,416
FUR COATS

781
00:38:53,500 --> 00:38:54,625
[Horacio] With what money?

782
00:38:55,125 --> 00:38:57,041
GOLD COINS

783
00:39:03,166 --> 00:39:05,375
Yiya's case is tied to an era of time

784
00:39:05,458 --> 00:39:08,208
when the pursuit
of easy money was deeply ingrained.

785
00:39:08,958 --> 00:39:10,958
[jazz music playing]

786
00:39:13,083 --> 00:39:13,958
EXCHANGE

787
00:39:14,041 --> 00:39:14,875
DOLLAR
GOLD

788
00:39:19,666 --> 00:39:21,583
[Candelaria] In Argentina
at the end of the '70s,

789
00:39:21,666 --> 00:39:23,958
there was a shift
in the way wealth was accumulated.

790
00:39:24,041 --> 00:39:26,333
The country moved
from a model based on industry

791
00:39:26,416 --> 00:39:28,625
to one focused on financial valorization,

792
00:39:28,708 --> 00:39:32,208
where wealth was increasingly generated
within the financial system itself.

793
00:39:32,291 --> 00:39:34,833
And it wasn't just
a change in economic policy

794
00:39:34,916 --> 00:39:39,291
but a transformation of the entire regime
that had been imposed by a dictatorship.

795
00:39:39,375 --> 00:39:41,458
This national reorganization process…

796
00:39:41,541 --> 00:39:43,166
LT. GEN. JORGE R. VIDELA
DE FACTO PRESIDENT

797
00:39:43,250 --> 00:39:45,291
…will demand time and effort.

798
00:39:45,375 --> 00:39:48,000
With that goal,
we will all fight tirelessly

799
00:39:48,916 --> 00:39:52,041
against subversive crime
until its total annihilation.

800
00:39:53,166 --> 00:39:55,375
The government of the Armed Forces…

801
00:39:55,458 --> 00:39:56,750
MINISTER OF ECONOMY 1976-1981

802
00:39:56,833 --> 00:40:00,625
…hasn't only eradicated
terrorist subversion,

803
00:40:00,708 --> 00:40:04,583
but has also now injected morality
to our economic life.

804
00:40:04,666 --> 00:40:05,916
[dramatic jazz music playing]

805
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:07,791
[thunder crashes]

806
00:40:10,833 --> 00:40:13,708
[Candelaria] The dictatorship could never
get inflation under control,

807
00:40:13,791 --> 00:40:15,916
and, in that kind of economic situation,

808
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:19,041
you have to keep your money moving,
or you'll lose purchasing power.

809
00:40:19,125 --> 00:40:20,250
BILLS
DOLLAR - BUY

810
00:40:20,333 --> 00:40:22,166
Back then, as it's always the case,

811
00:40:22,250 --> 00:40:24,333
if you had the capital,
you had to find ways

812
00:40:24,416 --> 00:40:27,666
to make it yield a bit more,
to be slightly better off.

813
00:40:33,875 --> 00:40:36,416
There was a major shift
in people's mindsets.

814
00:40:36,500 --> 00:40:40,333
Before, people were in a system where
you had to work in order to earn money.

815
00:40:40,416 --> 00:40:43,125
Then, we moved to a system where
you needed money to make money.

816
00:40:43,208 --> 00:40:45,541
Why? Because money began
working on its own.

817
00:40:46,625 --> 00:40:48,541
This gamble was a weekly thing.

818
00:40:48,625 --> 00:40:52,041
You could put your money in for three,
two, five days, however long you wanted.

819
00:40:52,125 --> 00:40:53,375
And the interest rates were…

820
00:40:55,958 --> 00:40:59,125
[Jorge] I'd get paid, put the money in
the bank, then I'd take out the interest.

821
00:40:59,208 --> 00:41:02,708
I lived off the interest. When
I'd get paid again, I'd put it back in.

822
00:41:02,791 --> 00:41:05,458
These are the survival tactics
of Argentina, right?

823
00:41:05,541 --> 00:41:07,125
We're all economists.

824
00:41:10,375 --> 00:41:11,791
[Yiya] Give me large bills, please.

825
00:41:13,041 --> 00:41:15,041
[Candelaria] And the whole concept
of easy money

826
00:41:15,125 --> 00:41:17,833
was as if the money
somehow kept multiplying by itself.

827
00:41:17,916 --> 00:41:21,125
So, it was a time when
many money pools were booming.

828
00:41:21,208 --> 00:41:25,250
People would put in their money to make
it grow through financial instruments.

829
00:41:25,333 --> 00:41:28,333
Sometimes they were legal, sometimes not.

830
00:41:28,416 --> 00:41:31,208
Sometimes they were based
on actual financial tools,

831
00:41:31,291 --> 00:41:33,833
and other times, they were outright scams.

832
00:41:37,375 --> 00:41:40,791
Yiya saw a window of opportunity
at the time,

833
00:41:40,875 --> 00:41:42,958
and made use of what she had.

834
00:41:44,750 --> 00:41:47,750
There's also the reality that,
in a patriarchal society,

835
00:41:47,833 --> 00:41:50,166
which was far more so than today,

836
00:41:50,250 --> 00:41:53,250
I am not sure that Yiya
could have convinced any men

837
00:41:53,333 --> 00:41:56,958
to entrust their money to a woman
to invest it in a money pool.

838
00:41:57,041 --> 00:41:59,875
She saw an opportunity there,
with her friends,

839
00:41:59,958 --> 00:42:04,250
who probably wouldn't have had access
to those men's investment circles.

840
00:42:04,333 --> 00:42:06,166
[gentle jazz music playing]

841
00:42:11,458 --> 00:42:13,166
Listen, ladies, I've got an acquaintance.

842
00:42:13,250 --> 00:42:16,000
Well, actually, he's a good friend,

843
00:42:16,083 --> 00:42:19,500
-He has a money pool that's very big.
-[Nilda] Really?

844
00:42:19,583 --> 00:42:21,916
[Virginia] Well actually,
what Yiya did in the '70s

845
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:23,500
was very much like a pyramid scheme.

846
00:42:23,583 --> 00:42:25,458
-I've got something to tell you.
-[Nilda] Yiya!

847
00:42:25,541 --> 00:42:27,250
No, but I'm not gonna tell you.
I better not.

848
00:42:27,333 --> 00:42:29,375
[Virginia] She'd take
a bit of money from them,

849
00:42:29,458 --> 00:42:31,041
then pay them a lot of interest.

850
00:42:31,125 --> 00:42:32,375
[Yiya] No one can know about it.

851
00:42:32,458 --> 00:42:34,708
[Virginia] And then she got them
to give her more money.

852
00:42:34,791 --> 00:42:38,958
[Yiya] It seems that next month,
the interest rate is going to triple.

853
00:42:39,875 --> 00:42:42,708
[Arona] My aunt Nilda had
some money set aside.

854
00:42:42,791 --> 00:42:47,416
so it's possible that when faced with the
chance of making higher profit margins,

855
00:42:47,500 --> 00:42:50,041
she might've made some sort of investment.

856
00:42:50,125 --> 00:42:52,166
We've all invested in something
at some point.

857
00:42:52,250 --> 00:42:54,541
-Nilda, tell the ladies how you did.
-Does it really triple?

858
00:42:54,625 --> 00:42:57,708
-[Nilda] Very well. Very well, ladies.
-What do you mean?

859
00:42:57,791 --> 00:42:59,125
-[Nilda] Twenty percent.
-Tell us!

860
00:42:59,208 --> 00:43:01,041
-Want me to order sandwiches?
-[Mema] Twenty?

861
00:43:01,125 --> 00:43:02,625
[Virginia] She always pampered them.

862
00:43:02,708 --> 00:43:06,041
She paid for them at the café,
she invited them to the theater.

863
00:43:06,125 --> 00:43:09,208
So they all felt that they
could absolutely trust her

864
00:43:09,291 --> 00:43:13,166
when it came to her financial abilities
and her economic stability.

865
00:43:13,250 --> 00:43:15,166
-They trusted Yiya.
-[Mema] The interest is crazy.

866
00:43:15,250 --> 00:43:18,041
Well, you should believe it,
but for that, you need trust.

867
00:43:18,125 --> 00:43:20,375
-[Mariana] My grandma was not gullible.
-You have to trust.

868
00:43:20,458 --> 00:43:24,375
I believe my grandmother loved Yiya
because she talked about her a lot.

869
00:43:24,875 --> 00:43:26,541
I think she kind of admired her.

870
00:43:26,625 --> 00:43:29,125
Yiya always had more things.

871
00:43:29,208 --> 00:43:31,500
More clothes, more coats, more…

872
00:43:31,583 --> 00:43:33,333
as if she knew more about life.

873
00:43:33,416 --> 00:43:34,708
Something like that.

874
00:43:34,791 --> 00:43:36,291
Like she had it all figured out.

875
00:43:36,375 --> 00:43:39,291
This isn't just for anyone,
only a trusted few.

876
00:43:40,291 --> 00:43:42,500
If it makes you anxious,
it might not be for you.

877
00:43:42,583 --> 00:43:45,375
If you want to make a profit,
you have to wait for the right time.

878
00:43:45,875 --> 00:43:48,041
[Martín] Back in the day,
the phones came with plugs.

879
00:43:48,125 --> 00:43:50,666
Well, Yiya had two phone plugs.

880
00:43:50,750 --> 00:43:52,750
One of them in the living room

881
00:43:52,833 --> 00:43:54,041
and one in the bedroom.

882
00:43:54,125 --> 00:43:55,583
But only one phone.

883
00:43:56,166 --> 00:43:58,041
-So she'd take the phone.
-[Yiya] Ten million.

884
00:43:58,125 --> 00:44:00,208
[Martín] And I was a kid
and I was curious.

885
00:44:00,291 --> 00:44:04,916
-"What is she saying that's so secretive?"
-Yes, look. Ten million. 25%.

886
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:07,791
[Martín] And I heard words
I didn't know the meaning of.

887
00:44:07,875 --> 00:44:09,333
Exactly. Hold on a minute.

888
00:44:09,416 --> 00:44:13,708
[Martín] "What on Earth is a money pool?
Is it a pool where you swim in money?"

889
00:44:18,666 --> 00:44:21,333
She'd hook them with the interest rates.
They were high.

890
00:44:21,416 --> 00:44:23,833
Then she would persuade them to reinvest.

891
00:44:25,708 --> 00:44:29,208
Put more money in,
and then make even more money off it.

892
00:44:29,291 --> 00:44:32,416
And they would keep bringing
her more money, each time.

893
00:44:36,041 --> 00:44:38,875
[Virginia] What's not clear to me is
whether she ever invested that money.

894
00:44:38,958 --> 00:44:41,708
Or perhaps it was the same money
she used to treat them,

895
00:44:41,791 --> 00:44:43,958
buy herself nice clothes,
or keep the scam running.

896
00:44:44,541 --> 00:44:46,625
My dad knew she was doing shady business.

897
00:44:46,708 --> 00:44:48,708
But then,

898
00:44:49,208 --> 00:44:51,333
my dad was just blinded by love.

899
00:44:52,083 --> 00:44:54,541
Her husband said,
"We lived with just enough."

900
00:44:54,625 --> 00:44:56,250
I mean, he never said, "Oh, yes,

901
00:44:56,333 --> 00:44:58,916
I actually asked Yiya
where she bought that coat,

902
00:44:59,000 --> 00:45:02,125
or when, or how she even got it."

903
00:45:02,208 --> 00:45:03,625
He seemed very naive to me.

904
00:45:04,791 --> 00:45:08,750
And people would also say, "Well, how
do you think she sustains that lifestyle?"

905
00:45:08,833 --> 00:45:11,791
"Because she knows about
these financial operations."

906
00:45:12,666 --> 00:45:14,416
She scammed people to make a living.

907
00:45:14,500 --> 00:45:17,833
Because she wanted to give,
she wanted to entertain,

908
00:45:17,916 --> 00:45:20,916
but she needed money,
and that's how she got it.

909
00:45:21,750 --> 00:45:23,125
She needed the money to maintain

910
00:45:23,208 --> 00:45:25,958
the aspirational consumption
expected at the time.

911
00:45:26,041 --> 00:45:28,083
It reflects a truth
about Argentina's middle class,

912
00:45:28,166 --> 00:45:30,750
the desire to belong
in circles where they really don't.

913
00:45:31,458 --> 00:45:34,958
We know that step two was to ask for more
money, and that's where it got tricky.

914
00:45:35,958 --> 00:45:41,333
There were many excuses. The money
would be available by, let's say November.

915
00:45:41,416 --> 00:45:42,708
But it wasn't.

916
00:45:42,791 --> 00:45:46,041
"Well, now it's actually only going
to be by the end of December."

917
00:45:46,875 --> 00:45:51,041
-Yes, yes, but that's in November.
-Yes, Yiya, I understand, but I…

918
00:45:52,208 --> 00:45:54,750
[Eloy] My mom had started
to pay for a trip to Europe.

919
00:45:54,833 --> 00:45:57,541
She was very excited.
She even got a passport.

920
00:45:57,625 --> 00:45:58,916
She had some money set aside

921
00:45:59,000 --> 00:46:01,791
that she hoped
to increase a bit to afford the trip.

922
00:46:01,875 --> 00:46:04,500
And that's why she handed
her money over for investments

923
00:46:04,583 --> 00:46:07,250
that promised more
than what a bank could offer.

924
00:46:07,333 --> 00:46:08,875
My mom told me, I knew.

925
00:46:08,958 --> 00:46:12,750
She kept a notebook where she wrote down
how much money she gave her.

926
00:46:13,666 --> 00:46:15,250
It was just a personal log.

927
00:46:15,333 --> 00:46:18,833
"Today, November 11th,
I gave Yiya Murano 200,000 pesos."

928
00:46:18,916 --> 00:46:19,791
In January…

929
00:46:19,875 --> 00:46:21,750
[Eloy] "She'll pay me back
on December 11th."

930
00:46:21,833 --> 00:46:22,708
DOUBLE

931
00:46:22,791 --> 00:46:24,708
"Interest is 20,000." Something like that.

932
00:46:24,791 --> 00:46:27,208
-It doubles.
-[Lelia] It doubles?

933
00:46:27,291 --> 00:46:28,166
It doubles.

934
00:46:28,250 --> 00:46:30,208
I gave it to them as evidence one time.

935
00:46:30,291 --> 00:46:31,916
DECEMBER 25%

936
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:34,125
[Eloy] It turned a profit
a couple of times.

937
00:46:34,208 --> 00:46:35,958
Until she stopped paying it back.
[chuckles]

938
00:46:36,708 --> 00:46:40,583
Oh, the things you'll be able
to do in Europe, Chicha. So much.

939
00:46:53,583 --> 00:46:55,583
[foreboding music playing]

940
00:47:02,916 --> 00:47:04,875
YIYA: PAID IN CYANIDE

941
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:08,208
The judge calls me
and he tells me I was right.

942
00:47:08,291 --> 00:47:10,416
THE POISONER: FORENSIC EXAMINATION
OF 2 EXHUMED CORPSES

943
00:47:10,500 --> 00:47:13,625
The conclusion came
from the examination of the corpses,

944
00:47:13,708 --> 00:47:16,541
as autopsies could no longer be performed.

945
00:47:17,041 --> 00:47:21,208
But they did a technical study
that finally determined

946
00:47:21,291 --> 00:47:24,041
that both had died from cyanide.

947
00:47:24,125 --> 00:47:26,166
THREE POISONINGS PROVEN
CYANIDE INVOLVED!

948
00:47:26,250 --> 00:47:30,000
When the bodies were exhumed, the issue
was that the levels were very low.

949
00:47:30,083 --> 00:47:32,750
Small traces of cyanide
were detected in a victim

950
00:47:32,833 --> 00:47:34,541
who had been buried for quite a long time.

951
00:47:34,625 --> 00:47:35,625
CORPSE - POTASSIUM CYANIDE

952
00:47:35,708 --> 00:47:37,458
But, based on the tests
that were carried out,

953
00:47:37,541 --> 00:47:41,833
we never had any doubt that she had
used cyanide on all three victims.

954
00:47:41,916 --> 00:47:43,291
THE POISONER: IS THE END NEAR?

955
00:47:43,375 --> 00:47:45,791
FORENSIC REPORTS TO DETERMINE
GUILT OF WELL-OFF KILLER

956
00:47:47,500 --> 00:47:49,083
[Horacio] Three or four years had passed.

957
00:47:49,166 --> 00:47:52,250
There were many appeals
and constant back-and-forth.

958
00:47:52,333 --> 00:47:56,041
But those are the normal timeframes
the justice system requires

959
00:47:56,125 --> 00:47:57,416
for the investigation,

960
00:47:57,500 --> 00:48:02,875
and for finding evidence before
being able to reach a final resolution.

961
00:48:02,958 --> 00:48:05,041
And once the investigation was over,

962
00:48:05,125 --> 00:48:08,666
and they considered that
there was nothing else to do,

963
00:48:08,750 --> 00:48:11,500
and all necessary evidence was gathered,

964
00:48:11,583 --> 00:48:13,666
they took it to the sentencing judge.

965
00:48:13,750 --> 00:48:15,750
THE LADY BEFORE THE JUDGE

966
00:48:15,833 --> 00:48:17,833
[dramatic music playing]

967
00:48:18,416 --> 00:48:20,458
[Virginia] There were no
oral arguments in her case,

968
00:48:20,541 --> 00:48:22,250
so everything had to be written.

969
00:48:22,750 --> 00:48:26,166
In the written proceedings,
the case moved to the plenary stage.

970
00:48:26,250 --> 00:48:28,333
The defense took part,
and, as expected in that moment…

971
00:48:28,416 --> 00:48:29,583
DEFENSE PREPARES TO FIGHT BACK

972
00:48:29,666 --> 00:48:30,916
…submitted its arguments.

973
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:33,958
[woman] I'm going to ask you a question
as her defense attorney.

974
00:48:34,041 --> 00:48:35,666
How does she seem to you?

975
00:48:35,750 --> 00:48:36,916
YIYA MURANO'S DEFENSE ATTORNEY

976
00:48:37,000 --> 00:48:38,000
She seems fine.

977
00:48:38,083 --> 00:48:42,083
I get the impression she's innocent.
That's why I'm involved in this case.

978
00:48:42,166 --> 00:48:43,666
DEFENSE
BOLLA APONTE DE MURANO

979
00:48:43,750 --> 00:48:45,291
DEFENSE ATTORNEY:
MARIO R. SOAJE PINTO

980
00:48:45,375 --> 00:48:47,333
DEFENDANT SYSTEMATICALLY
DENIED COMMITTING HOMICIDE

981
00:48:47,416 --> 00:48:51,458
[woman] Mr. Miguel Botello, what is your
personal opinion on Mrs. Murano's case?

982
00:48:51,541 --> 00:48:54,708
In this specific poisoning case,
it's not only necessary

983
00:48:54,791 --> 00:48:57,458
to find traces of the poison,
if there was any…

984
00:48:57,541 --> 00:48:58,875
NO ALKALINE CYANIDE WAS SEIZED

985
00:48:58,958 --> 00:49:00,750
…but also to find a way to prove

986
00:49:00,833 --> 00:49:03,666
that the defendant was actually
the one who poisoned the victim.

987
00:49:03,750 --> 00:49:05,666
UNKNOWN HOW CYANIDE INGESTION
COULD HAVE OCCURRED

988
00:49:05,750 --> 00:49:08,750
Besides, there are practically
no witnesses to this crime,

989
00:49:08,833 --> 00:49:11,458
so the court will have to rely
on circumstantial evidence.

990
00:49:13,625 --> 00:49:16,458
Tell me, Mr. Kepsely, is it possible

991
00:49:16,541 --> 00:49:18,708
to make any kind of judgment
about the defendant?

992
00:49:18,791 --> 00:49:21,750
The fact that a person is found
with poison in their viscera

993
00:49:21,833 --> 00:49:24,500
is not enough information
to claim in any way

994
00:49:24,583 --> 00:49:27,208
that the person being prosecuted
is responsible for those deaths.

995
00:49:27,291 --> 00:49:28,750
THE PRESENCE OF CYANIDE DOES NOT MEAN

996
00:49:28,833 --> 00:49:30,791
THE SUBJECT DIED
FROM INGESTION OF ALKALINE CYANIDE

997
00:49:30,875 --> 00:49:32,291
DEFENSE ATTORNEY
REITERATES INNOCENCE

998
00:49:32,375 --> 00:49:35,875
The defense said two things regarding
the cyanide. These women were smokers.

999
00:49:35,958 --> 00:49:37,833
And some studies have suggested

1000
00:49:37,916 --> 00:49:41,583
that certain substances in cigarettes
decompose in the body, producing cyanide.

1001
00:49:41,666 --> 00:49:43,291
TRACES OF HYDROCYANIC ACID
FOUND IN SMOKERS

1002
00:49:43,375 --> 00:49:45,875
Alternatively,
the natural process of decomposition

1003
00:49:45,958 --> 00:49:47,875
may cause the body
to release chemical substances…

1004
00:49:47,958 --> 00:49:49,333
VERY ABUNDANT LAYERS OF BACTERIA

1005
00:49:49,416 --> 00:49:51,541
…that can be interpreted
as decomposition of cyanide.

1006
00:49:51,625 --> 00:49:52,791
DETECTED IN POSTMORTEM TISSUES

1007
00:49:52,875 --> 00:49:54,500
MICROQUANTITIES OF ALKALINE CYANIDE

1008
00:49:54,583 --> 00:49:55,750
MURANO WAITS ALONE IN EZEIZA

1009
00:49:55,833 --> 00:49:57,541
29 MONTHS AFTER TRIPLE POISONING:
SENTENCING

1010
00:49:57,625 --> 00:50:00,250
That doubt about the cyanide prevailed,

1011
00:50:00,333 --> 00:50:03,791
a point the defense had
skillfully introduced.

1012
00:50:03,875 --> 00:50:06,125
What the judges said was in dubio pro reo,

1013
00:50:06,208 --> 00:50:09,291
meaning that doubt works
in favor of the accused.

1014
00:50:09,916 --> 00:50:12,791
The justice system follows
the principle that it's better

1015
00:50:12,875 --> 00:50:16,208
for a criminal to go free than
for an innocent to be imprisoned.

1016
00:50:17,333 --> 00:50:19,916
[Kepsely] Because, otherwise,
reversing the burden of proof

1017
00:50:20,000 --> 00:50:24,458
could truly endanger our entire
justice system, nationally.

1018
00:50:24,541 --> 00:50:26,750
YIYA: IMMINENT JUDICIAL DECISION

1019
00:50:26,833 --> 00:50:28,208
THE PEOPLE ASK FOR HER FREEDOM!

1020
00:50:28,291 --> 00:50:30,291
[Horacio] The sentencing judge

1021
00:50:30,375 --> 00:50:32,916
considered that there
was not enough evidence,

1022
00:50:33,916 --> 00:50:35,291
and acquitted her.

1023
00:50:38,458 --> 00:50:41,583
MARÍA DE LAS MERCEDES BOLLA APONTE
DE MURANO ACQUITTED OF ALL CHARGES

1024
00:50:42,500 --> 00:50:47,125
YIYA IS FREE

1025
00:50:48,708 --> 00:50:51,166
[man] We're witnessing the moment
when one of the ships

1026
00:50:51,750 --> 00:50:55,083
bringing back to the continent
the Argentine soldiers

1027
00:50:55,166 --> 00:50:59,083
who fought for our sovereignty in
the Malvinas Islands approaches the port.

1028
00:50:59,166 --> 00:51:00,166
3 YEARS AFTER MEMA'S DEATH

1029
00:51:00,250 --> 00:51:02,958
[Martín] She had spent
three years in jail by then.

1030
00:51:03,041 --> 00:51:05,208
I was 16 years old.

1031
00:51:06,166 --> 00:51:08,833
-A total of 472 troops…
-[Martín] I was smoking.

1032
00:51:09,541 --> 00:51:11,875
-…have arrived in the southern Patagonian…
-Eat it all, okay?

1033
00:51:11,958 --> 00:51:14,083
-[Martín] I was going out.
-[TV] …transporting soldiers…

1034
00:51:14,166 --> 00:51:15,708
-[Martín] She got home.
-[door closes]

1035
00:51:15,791 --> 00:51:18,416
-And said, "Aren't you giving me a hug?"
-[TV] …back after so long.

1036
00:51:18,500 --> 00:51:21,166
-[Yiya] I'm going out. Back later.
-[Martín] I said, "What happened?"

1037
00:51:21,250 --> 00:51:23,916
-"Did you escape or something?"
-[Yiya] Did you put cheese in it?

1038
00:51:24,000 --> 00:51:25,791
-[Ignacia] Yes.
-[Martín] "The truth came out."

1039
00:51:25,875 --> 00:51:27,416
-Thanks.
-[Martín] "I didn't kill them."

1040
00:51:27,500 --> 00:51:30,041
I said, "What terrible luck you had."

1041
00:51:30,666 --> 00:51:35,208
"I mean, three women you owed money to
all died in the same month?"

1042
00:51:37,166 --> 00:51:38,750
[Antonio] Will you be back late?

1043
00:51:39,750 --> 00:51:42,416
-I have to meet my lawyer, make calls.
-[Martín] "You never loved me."

1044
00:51:42,500 --> 00:51:44,666
-"You were always a bad son."
-[Yiya] Don't be a pain.

1045
00:51:44,750 --> 00:51:46,916
[Martín] "Of course
you'd say something like that."

1046
00:51:49,083 --> 00:51:50,458
What did I think?

1047
00:51:51,750 --> 00:51:54,291
"God damn it. Here we go again."

1048
00:51:54,375 --> 00:51:56,583
Because Yiya started doing
the same things.

1049
00:51:56,666 --> 00:51:58,333
She was on the phone again.

1050
00:51:58,416 --> 00:52:01,500
She was seeing Cantón
more and more frequently.

1051
00:52:01,583 --> 00:52:03,583
[jazz music playing]

1052
00:52:04,416 --> 00:52:06,625
[Martín] Yiya did not change
in the slightest.

1053
00:52:10,125 --> 00:52:13,750
What I remember from that time is that
someone from the police was furious.

1054
00:52:13,833 --> 00:52:16,791
He'd carried out a thorough investigation,
gathered plenty of evidence,

1055
00:52:16,875 --> 00:52:18,458
and he was angry about it.

1056
00:52:19,250 --> 00:52:23,041
I was angry because they weren't being
completely honest about some things,

1057
00:52:23,125 --> 00:52:26,958
and I knew very well that she had done it,

1058
00:52:27,041 --> 00:52:28,625
yet she'd been acquitted.

1059
00:52:29,458 --> 00:52:30,875
I was feeling scared.

1060
00:52:30,958 --> 00:52:33,583
But, above all,
I was scared for my mother.

1061
00:52:34,166 --> 00:52:35,375
[doorbell buzzes]

1062
00:52:35,458 --> 00:52:37,708
[Mariana] I was afraid
she might show up at my house,

1063
00:52:37,791 --> 00:52:41,416
or that she'd poison my mother
without us even realizing it.

1064
00:52:42,416 --> 00:52:46,083
And that it was crucial she be locked up,
because she was dangerous.

1065
00:52:46,166 --> 00:52:48,625
GUILTY OR INNOCENT?
THE POISONER WALKS FREE

1066
00:52:49,250 --> 00:52:51,541
FREE NOW, BUT THE CASE REMAINS OPEN

1067
00:52:51,625 --> 00:52:55,583
The prosecutor was forced to appeal. The
case was sent to a higher jurisdiction.

1068
00:52:55,666 --> 00:52:58,791
THE POISONER: BACK IN FRONT OF THE JUDGE

1069
00:53:06,916 --> 00:53:09,666
-[producer] There you go. Okay.
-All right. I think we should hide this.

1070
00:53:10,583 --> 00:53:13,083
Yiya Murano had been acquitted
in the first trial.

1071
00:53:13,166 --> 00:53:15,875
It had been proven that she asked
to borrow money from her friends.

1072
00:53:15,958 --> 00:53:17,125
FORMER JUDGE

1073
00:53:17,208 --> 00:53:20,666
When the time came to pay them back,
she'd invite them for tea

1074
00:53:20,750 --> 00:53:22,916
and they died shortly after.

1075
00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:24,208
That was clear.

1076
00:53:24,291 --> 00:53:26,666
But we had to prove there was poison.

1077
00:53:27,416 --> 00:53:29,500
Because if there wasn't any poison,

1078
00:53:30,666 --> 00:53:33,375
then it was an invitation to tea.

1079
00:53:33,458 --> 00:53:36,833
Finding the poison was my mission.

1080
00:53:37,625 --> 00:53:42,875
Which basically consisted of proving
that what had been found in the bodies…

1081
00:53:42,958 --> 00:53:44,000
HYDROCYANIC ACID

1082
00:53:44,083 --> 00:53:47,250
…was the final stage in a chain
of several transformations…

1083
00:53:47,333 --> 00:53:49,541
VISCERAL MATERIAL - DISTILLATION
CYANIDE SEPARATED

1084
00:53:49,625 --> 00:53:51,166
RESIDUES: CONVERSION TO THIOCYANATE

1085
00:53:51,250 --> 00:53:52,958
…originating from the poison.

1086
00:53:53,666 --> 00:53:58,875
There were chemical compounds that could
only result from the poison's degradation.

1087
00:53:58,958 --> 00:53:59,958
CYANIDE
THIOCYANATE

1088
00:54:00,041 --> 00:54:02,666
How did she manage to get it
into the victims' bodies?

1089
00:54:02,750 --> 00:54:03,708
Well…

1090
00:54:05,250 --> 00:54:09,125
everything indicates
that it was through a liquid medium.

1091
00:54:10,541 --> 00:54:13,291
Today, I'm going to teach you
how to prepare a lovely tea

1092
00:54:13,375 --> 00:54:15,500
so you get to delight your friends.

1093
00:54:16,000 --> 00:54:18,208
First, we'll get the teacup.

1094
00:54:18,291 --> 00:54:21,375
We're now going to put one teaspoon

1095
00:54:21,958 --> 00:54:24,166
per person inside the teapot.

1096
00:54:25,708 --> 00:54:28,916
We'll pour it into the teacup
using a strainer.

1097
00:54:29,875 --> 00:54:31,625
And the final touch.

1098
00:54:31,708 --> 00:54:33,291
SODIUM CYANIDE

1099
00:54:34,916 --> 00:54:36,291
Three drops.

1100
00:54:37,916 --> 00:54:38,833
Four.

1101
00:54:41,666 --> 00:54:43,291
-[Yiya] How do you feel?
-Not well at all.

1102
00:54:43,375 --> 00:54:44,833
My stomach is really hurting.

1103
00:54:45,458 --> 00:54:46,875
[Yiya] What did you eat yesterday?

1104
00:54:46,958 --> 00:54:50,000
In Nilda's case,
she could've given it to her in the tea,

1105
00:54:50,083 --> 00:54:54,166
because Nilda was feeling unwell,
and she was taking care of her.

1106
00:54:54,250 --> 00:54:57,500
She couldn't drink anything but tea,
so maybe…

1107
00:54:58,000 --> 00:55:00,583
-Drink it, Nilda.
-[Nilda] Yes.

1108
00:55:01,458 --> 00:55:06,333
Since Nilda was diabetic and had been
hospitalized and sick some time earlier,

1109
00:55:06,416 --> 00:55:10,041
her illness and subsequent death
were not really that surprising.

1110
00:55:10,125 --> 00:55:11,833
DECEMBER 1978
SHE FELT FAINT

1111
00:55:11,916 --> 00:55:13,458
[Horacio] Nilda had recently suffered

1112
00:55:13,541 --> 00:55:15,625
a pretty severe health breakdown
three months earlier…

1113
00:55:15,708 --> 00:55:16,750
HOSPITALIZED IN A CLINIC

1114
00:55:16,833 --> 00:55:18,291
…which did not cause her death.

1115
00:55:18,375 --> 00:55:20,291
AFTER REGAINING CONSCIOUSNESS,
PATIENT SAID

1116
00:55:20,375 --> 00:55:22,541
SHE HAD HAD TWO DRINKS

1117
00:55:22,625 --> 00:55:24,000
WITH HER FRIEND "YIYA"

1118
00:55:24,083 --> 00:55:27,375
It was discovered
that Mrs. Gamba had a prior incident,

1119
00:55:27,458 --> 00:55:29,041
a possible earlier poisoning.

1120
00:55:29,125 --> 00:55:30,458
MRS. MURANO TOLD HER TO TAKE

1121
00:55:30,541 --> 00:55:32,416
It might have been an attempt
to test the dosage.

1122
00:55:32,500 --> 00:55:35,333
It failed, because
it only caused her minor harm.

1123
00:55:35,416 --> 00:55:36,291
A TABLET OR A PILL

1124
00:55:37,375 --> 00:55:42,541
And her family took her to Punta del Este,
because it was the summer at that time.

1125
00:55:42,625 --> 00:55:44,333
She recovered perfectly well.

1126
00:55:44,416 --> 00:55:46,541
She came back sun-soaked
and in great health.

1127
00:55:46,625 --> 00:55:47,500
FEBRUARY 11

1128
00:55:47,583 --> 00:55:48,458
She meets with Yiya…

1129
00:55:48,541 --> 00:55:49,416
SUFFERS NEW DISCOMFORT

1130
00:55:49,500 --> 00:55:51,916
…asks for her money back, and dies.

1131
00:55:53,583 --> 00:55:55,500
And when she came back
to Buenos Aires,

1132
00:55:55,583 --> 00:55:58,375
well, that nice lady found the right dose.

1133
00:56:02,250 --> 00:56:04,833
Of course,
Yiya knew perfectly well that cyanide

1134
00:56:04,916 --> 00:56:07,041
caused sudden and immediate death.

1135
00:56:07,125 --> 00:56:11,583
She couldn't administer it the way
that they said she did administer it.

1136
00:56:11,666 --> 00:56:14,666
If so, Nilda would've died with her there.

1137
00:56:15,333 --> 00:56:18,500
She was well aware
that she needed to find her dead.

1138
00:56:19,083 --> 00:56:21,208
After all your debts,
all the problems you've had.

1139
00:56:21,291 --> 00:56:24,916
-And now you're spending all this money.
-[man] You shouldn't worry about this…

1140
00:56:25,000 --> 00:56:27,458
[Virginia] In Ayala's case,
Yiya had been having tea with her.

1141
00:56:27,541 --> 00:56:29,291
MRS. BOLLA DE MURANO

1142
00:56:29,375 --> 00:56:30,500
WAS WITH THE DECEASED

1143
00:56:30,583 --> 00:56:31,541
Once she leaves…

1144
00:56:31,625 --> 00:56:32,500
THAT SAME DAY

1145
00:56:32,583 --> 00:56:36,708
…she sets up an obviously staged plan
to go to the theater,

1146
00:56:36,791 --> 00:56:40,083
involving three people
who didn't know each other.

1147
00:56:40,166 --> 00:56:41,583
Before going to the theater,

1148
00:56:41,666 --> 00:56:45,541
she made sure she would have an alibi

1149
00:56:45,625 --> 00:56:48,375
by going to Ayala's house,
ringing her doorbell,

1150
00:56:48,458 --> 00:56:51,958
and acting surprised in front of everyone,
saying, "She's not answering. How weird."

1151
00:56:52,041 --> 00:56:53,291
"She didn't come. That's odd."

1152
00:56:53,375 --> 00:56:56,333
So that way, if they were to testify,

1153
00:56:56,416 --> 00:56:59,750
they'd say that she seemed surprised
by her friend's absence,

1154
00:56:59,833 --> 00:57:02,875
though she knew perfectly well that
she was dying, or was already dead.

1155
00:57:02,958 --> 00:57:06,416
LEFTOVER FISH AND PASTRIES

1156
00:57:06,500 --> 00:57:08,500
On the table, there were still leftovers

1157
00:57:08,583 --> 00:57:10,875
from the pastries
that they both had eaten.

1158
00:57:11,375 --> 00:57:14,125
Later on, the story got out,

1159
00:57:14,208 --> 00:57:17,833
and people began saying
that she poisoned them with pastries.

1160
00:57:30,541 --> 00:57:33,541
Yes, I went there afterward,
to pick up some of my mom's things.

1161
00:57:33,625 --> 00:57:36,875
And people laugh about this,
but there were pastries in the fridge,

1162
00:57:36,958 --> 00:57:38,500
and I think I had one.

1163
00:57:38,583 --> 00:57:39,541
And…

1164
00:57:40,166 --> 00:57:42,416
-[producer] Yiya's pastries?
-Yeah.

1165
00:57:44,041 --> 00:57:47,416
Well, listen,
when I went to her apartment,

1166
00:57:47,500 --> 00:57:49,916
it was shortly after my mother's death.

1167
00:57:50,000 --> 00:57:52,625
Nobody had a clue
my mom had been poisoned.

1168
00:57:55,291 --> 00:57:59,291
Like in many criminal cases, there's
this one thing the public obsesses over.

1169
00:57:59,375 --> 00:58:03,916
Here, they never let go
of this story about the pastries.

1170
00:58:04,000 --> 00:58:07,708
But the truth is, we don't know for sure
if the poison was in the pastries.

1171
00:58:07,791 --> 00:58:09,958
In fact, we believe that it wasn't.

1172
00:58:10,916 --> 00:58:13,291
But of course,
because they saw the pastries

1173
00:58:13,375 --> 00:58:15,250
and the truth was never told.

1174
00:58:15,875 --> 00:58:19,708
The truth wasn't revealed
about how Yiya poisoned people.

1175
00:58:22,875 --> 00:58:26,708
We could say that the pastries
were a means she'd use

1176
00:58:26,791 --> 00:58:31,500
so that they would just keep eating,
and overindulge,

1177
00:58:31,583 --> 00:58:36,083
so they would then need
to drink something to feel better.

1178
00:58:37,541 --> 00:58:40,041
We're talking about people
in their sixties.

1179
00:58:40,125 --> 00:58:44,416
So, you know, eating too many pastries
doesn't usually agree with them.

1180
00:58:44,500 --> 00:58:47,875
So she'd say to them,
"If you're feeling unwell,

1181
00:58:47,958 --> 00:58:51,875
I have a special herbal tincture
that's really great for digestion."

1182
00:58:51,958 --> 00:58:54,333
"You should take it,
it'll make you feel better."

1183
00:58:55,166 --> 00:58:58,041
It was just like now,
when you visit a friend and they say,

1184
00:58:58,125 --> 00:59:00,625
"You know, I've been taking
this oregano oil, and…"

1185
00:59:00,708 --> 00:59:03,458
It's something we all do.
"Drink it, it's good for you."

1186
00:59:04,291 --> 00:59:06,125
Her modus operandi was

1187
00:59:06,208 --> 00:59:09,916
to make sure to fill them up
with things that wouldn't agree with them

1188
00:59:12,833 --> 00:59:14,416
so they'd feel sick

1189
00:59:15,500 --> 00:59:20,250
and then, they'd ingest
her herbal tincture alone, at night.

1190
00:59:20,833 --> 00:59:25,541
Go back there,
and retrieve the IOU slip she had signed,

1191
00:59:25,625 --> 00:59:27,500
so she wouldn't have to pay any debt.

1192
00:59:27,583 --> 00:59:30,791
-Mema.
-And nothing happened here.

1193
00:59:31,375 --> 00:59:33,375
That would explain why,
in Venturini's case,

1194
00:59:33,458 --> 00:59:36,333
she needed to go into her apartment
to retrieve the dropper bottle.

1195
00:59:36,416 --> 00:59:38,750
-[Mirtha] And now we're going to welcome…
-This is mine.

1196
00:59:38,833 --> 00:59:40,750
[Horacio] She didn't know
if she'd find her dead.

1197
00:59:40,833 --> 00:59:43,000
She'd been there
and had given her pastries,

1198
00:59:43,083 --> 00:59:47,208
but she had more with her,
just in case it hadn't worked.

1199
00:59:49,333 --> 00:59:53,333
It was a criminal plan that wasn't
as simple as "Eat this pastry and die."

1200
00:59:53,416 --> 00:59:57,125
No, not at all. No, because
that way, they'd incriminate her.

1201
01:00:00,833 --> 01:00:05,208
Yiya had such an inflated ego
that she thought she'd get away with it.

1202
01:00:06,416 --> 01:00:08,583
She thought she was smarter
than the judges.

1203
01:00:13,333 --> 01:00:18,250
The body of evidence, which was
extensive, precise, and compelling,

1204
01:00:18,958 --> 01:00:22,000
left no doubt whatsoever
regarding the fact

1205
01:00:22,083 --> 01:00:24,958
that the acts of poisoning

1206
01:00:25,708 --> 01:00:28,250
committed by Yiya had indeed occurred.

1207
01:00:28,333 --> 01:00:31,375
The code says, "Poisoning, life sentence."

1208
01:00:31,875 --> 01:00:35,250
So we sentenced her to life in prison.

1209
01:00:35,750 --> 01:00:37,875
MAX SENTENCE
LIFE IMPRISONMENT - OPINION UPHELD

1210
01:00:37,958 --> 01:00:39,833
LIFE IMPRISONMENT FOR "YIYA"

1211
01:00:39,916 --> 01:00:41,375
"YIYA" GOES TO JAIL

1212
01:00:41,458 --> 01:00:44,958
Due to some judicial technicalities,
she was released at some point,

1213
01:00:45,041 --> 01:00:47,208
but then, the truth prevailed.

1214
01:00:47,291 --> 01:00:48,458
YIYA IS IN JAIL

1215
01:00:48,541 --> 01:00:50,833
[Martín] Then they told her,
"Yiya, it's over."

1216
01:00:50,916 --> 01:00:55,041
"Go inside, lock the door and
don't make plans for the next 15 years."

1217
01:00:55,125 --> 01:00:56,375
[dramatic music playing]

1218
01:01:00,333 --> 01:01:03,291
[Massoni] One day I went to that prison
for another reason.

1219
01:01:03,375 --> 01:01:08,250
and I found out that she was
the boss of an entire cell block.

1220
01:01:10,208 --> 01:01:12,916
All the prisoners were fascinated by her.

1221
01:01:13,416 --> 01:01:17,083
Especially because of all that
she'd gotten them once she got out.

1222
01:01:17,166 --> 01:01:19,416
"…IF I'M CONVICTED,
I'M NOT GOING TO SHOOT MYSELF"

1223
01:01:19,500 --> 01:01:22,500
"EVEN IF A HUNDRED YEARS GO BY,
I'LL COME OUT WITH MY HEAD HIGH"

1224
01:01:22,583 --> 01:01:24,666
"I SWEAR ON JESUS CHRIST"

1225
01:01:27,250 --> 01:01:29,541
[Massoni] She was
an extraordinary character.

1226
01:01:29,625 --> 01:01:32,125
She could convince anyone of anything.

1227
01:01:33,958 --> 01:01:35,750
-A complete psychopath.
-[Yiya] Sweetie!

1228
01:01:35,833 --> 01:01:37,791
Yes, you. Come here now.

1229
01:01:37,875 --> 01:01:40,041
-[Massoni] And with no morals whatsoever.
-[Yiya] Come.

1230
01:01:40,541 --> 01:01:42,083
Come here, come closer.

1231
01:01:47,375 --> 01:01:49,375
[melancholy music playing]

1232
01:01:57,708 --> 01:02:02,250
[Martín] One day, my dad told me,
in a voice barely over a whisper,

1233
01:02:03,833 --> 01:02:05,291
"She was convicted."

1234
01:02:07,291 --> 01:02:09,625
"She was sentenced to life in prison."

1235
01:02:11,333 --> 01:02:13,125
"What are we gonna do now, son?"

1236
01:02:13,208 --> 01:02:14,833
"MY MOTHER WAS CONVICTED
IN A STRANGE WAY"

1237
01:02:14,916 --> 01:02:16,041
[Martín] I had to lie.

1238
01:02:16,125 --> 01:02:17,791
"MY MOTHER CAN'T EVEN COOK A STEAK"

1239
01:02:17,875 --> 01:02:22,208
I said that I believed in Yiya's innocence
to soothe my dad.

1240
01:02:25,250 --> 01:02:29,750
He passed away
still maintaining that Yiya was innocent.

1241
01:02:31,333 --> 01:02:32,708
It killed my father.

1242
01:02:40,416 --> 01:02:43,291
My father has influenced
my life immensely.

1243
01:02:43,791 --> 01:02:46,541
When I was eight years old,
he took me to see a movie

1244
01:02:46,625 --> 01:02:47,916
with Jean-Paul Belmondo.

1245
01:02:48,875 --> 01:02:51,041
The movie was called Animal.

1246
01:02:51,125 --> 01:02:55,333
And at the same time, he took on
the role of his own stunt double.

1247
01:02:55,416 --> 01:02:56,541
[funky music playing]

1248
01:02:57,166 --> 01:03:00,958
[Martín] And, in one scene,
I saw him jumping out of a helicopter

1249
01:03:01,041 --> 01:03:03,416
and I asked,
"Dad, is that a camera trick?"

1250
01:03:04,250 --> 01:03:07,083
"No, the person doing it
is a stunt double."

1251
01:03:07,166 --> 01:03:09,125
[crew cheering]

1252
01:03:09,208 --> 01:03:12,833
"They are the ones who do the scenes
that involve risks for an actor,

1253
01:03:14,875 --> 01:03:16,666
so that the actor doesn't take risks."

1254
01:03:16,750 --> 01:03:18,166
"Besides, they don't know how to."

1255
01:03:18,250 --> 01:03:19,125
No!

1256
01:03:19,208 --> 01:03:22,416
[Martín] I became obsessed. I was hooked.

1257
01:03:22,500 --> 01:03:25,041
You know the classic question
in elementary school?

1258
01:03:25,541 --> 01:03:27,166
"What do you want to be
when you grow up?"

1259
01:03:27,750 --> 01:03:30,375
And I'd say, "Stunt double,"
and they'd laugh at me.

1260
01:03:31,625 --> 01:03:32,833
Who's laughing now?

1261
01:03:33,416 --> 01:03:35,583
-[tires squeal]
-[Martín] Go ahead and laugh now.

1262
01:03:35,666 --> 01:03:38,916
I've got more than a thousand hours
of work on TV as a stunt double.

1263
01:03:39,000 --> 01:03:41,333
I've also worked on movies abroad.

1264
01:03:41,833 --> 01:03:44,916
I was the first stunt double in Argentina

1265
01:03:45,000 --> 01:03:48,083
to set myself on fire from head to toe
on television,

1266
01:03:48,166 --> 01:03:51,416
with my face uncovered
and without protection.

1267
01:03:51,500 --> 01:03:53,166
[camera shutter clicking]

1268
01:03:56,041 --> 01:03:59,083
In fact, right here,
while we were shooting this,

1269
01:03:59,166 --> 01:04:04,291
I met someone who I used to work with
at Channel 9, more than 30 years ago.

1270
01:04:05,875 --> 01:04:06,833
[producer] Take a seat.

1271
01:04:07,875 --> 01:04:09,083
Eyes on the board.

1272
01:04:13,416 --> 01:04:15,416
Well, I worked with
Martín Murano at Channel 9…

1273
01:04:15,500 --> 01:04:16,750
FILM CREW MEMBER

1274
01:04:16,833 --> 01:04:21,916
…in the '90s,
on some of Channel 9's scripted shows.

1275
01:04:22,500 --> 01:04:25,208
No, he'd introduce himself
as Martín Murano.

1276
01:04:25,291 --> 01:04:27,958
He carried that name with him.

1277
01:04:28,458 --> 01:04:31,000
I mean, everyone knew
he was Martín Murano.

1278
01:04:31,083 --> 01:04:32,541
Yeah, he was a stunt double,

1279
01:04:32,625 --> 01:04:36,125
and he was a fight choreographer
and stuff like that.

1280
01:04:36,625 --> 01:04:40,083
He was good at his job. In fact,
I took some pictures of him working.

1281
01:04:40,166 --> 01:04:42,125
He didn't know. I've got 'em somewhere.

1282
01:04:42,208 --> 01:04:44,416
This is a scene at Channel 9,

1283
01:04:45,250 --> 01:04:49,625
and here's Martín Murano
teaching an actress how to kick.

1284
01:04:49,708 --> 01:04:51,083
[blow landing sound effect]

1285
01:04:51,166 --> 01:04:52,500
[Martín] For me, TV is my life.

1286
01:04:52,583 --> 01:04:57,000
Today, despite being here,
retelling a very sad story,

1287
01:04:57,083 --> 01:05:01,833
having that little machine there
right in front of me

1288
01:05:02,833 --> 01:05:04,250
brings me back to life.

1289
01:05:05,166 --> 01:05:07,666
Okay, okay, see you later.

1290
01:05:10,750 --> 01:05:15,291
[Martín] Yiya, during that time,
would be watching TV,

1291
01:05:15,375 --> 01:05:18,500
and when she saw
that the credits of the show said,

1292
01:05:18,583 --> 01:05:20,416
"Guest appearance, Martín Murano…"

1293
01:05:20,500 --> 01:05:21,416
Sweetie!

1294
01:05:21,500 --> 01:05:23,833
Look, he's my son!

1295
01:05:23,916 --> 01:05:26,666
-[Martín] She'd call the other inmates.
-[Yiya] He's friends with Romay.

1296
01:05:26,750 --> 01:05:29,125
[Martín] She'd tell them, "That's my son."

1297
01:05:29,208 --> 01:05:30,250
Never.

1298
01:05:30,333 --> 01:05:31,500
[Martín] And she'd exaggerate.

1299
01:05:31,583 --> 01:05:35,291
She'd say to them,
"He's a top executive at Channel 9."

1300
01:05:35,375 --> 01:05:37,166
-[woman] Really?
-[Martín] "When I get out…"

1301
01:05:37,250 --> 01:05:38,333
[Yiya] He's on the right.

1302
01:05:38,416 --> 01:05:39,708
[Martín] "He's going to help me,

1303
01:05:39,791 --> 01:05:42,291
and I'll be able to get you
all sorts of things."

1304
01:05:42,375 --> 01:05:43,333
"You'll see."

1305
01:05:44,166 --> 01:05:45,791
[Yiya] Yes, he's very well known.

1306
01:05:46,875 --> 01:05:50,000
No, I don't know about Yiya or Martín.
I didn't follow the case.

1307
01:05:50,083 --> 01:05:52,583
But when we were working
and would bump into each other,

1308
01:05:52,666 --> 01:05:54,625
he would comment
that he was writing a book.

1309
01:05:56,375 --> 01:05:57,583
While we were shooting,

1310
01:05:58,791 --> 01:06:01,625
I'd just sit in the car and start writing.

1311
01:06:02,708 --> 01:06:04,083
"What are you writing?"

1312
01:06:05,791 --> 01:06:07,791
I'd reply,
"You won't believe me if I tell you."

1313
01:06:08,625 --> 01:06:10,583
[woman] It's so silent until I get there.

1314
01:06:10,666 --> 01:06:12,666
I'm here with Mr. Martín Murano.

1315
01:06:12,750 --> 01:06:13,916
-Hi, Martín.
-Nice to meet you.

1316
01:06:14,000 --> 01:06:15,916
-It's a pleasure. How are you?
-[Martín] I'm good.

1317
01:06:16,000 --> 01:06:19,291
[woman] He'll tell us about his mom
and the book he's about to publish.

1318
01:06:19,375 --> 01:06:24,333
Martín, you're so young, 27 years old.
So, you were a kid when all this happened.

1319
01:06:24,416 --> 01:06:27,500
[Martín] Yes, I was 12, almost 13.

1320
01:06:27,583 --> 01:06:31,250
[woman] Up to that moment, how was your
relationship with your mother, Martín?

1321
01:06:31,333 --> 01:06:33,083
It may be shocking for me to say this,

1322
01:06:33,166 --> 01:06:37,416
but we've never had a good relationship,
honestly, not even when I was a kid.

1323
01:06:37,500 --> 01:06:40,750
Well, it's like
I say in the book, you know?

1324
01:06:40,833 --> 01:06:44,416
Sometimes it's interesting,
the things you get used to.

1325
01:06:44,500 --> 01:06:46,208
[man] What made you write the book?

1326
01:06:46,291 --> 01:06:48,625
Every time I said my name,
the inevitable question was,

1327
01:06:48,708 --> 01:06:50,625
"Are you related to Yiya Murano?"

1328
01:06:51,333 --> 01:06:54,333
Every time I left the house,
there were journalists at the door.

1329
01:06:54,916 --> 01:06:56,541
Many malicious comments.

1330
01:06:57,708 --> 01:06:58,833
And even if

1331
01:06:59,333 --> 01:07:01,291
you accept them,
you never get used to them.

1332
01:07:01,375 --> 01:07:02,250
It's upsetting.

1333
01:07:02,333 --> 01:07:04,500
-Yes, but you also might think--
-[Martín] That's it.

1334
01:07:04,583 --> 01:07:08,041
People will soon forget.
In a country with no memory like this one,

1335
01:07:08,125 --> 01:07:10,625
you're probably reminding people
of who Yiya Murano is,

1336
01:07:10,708 --> 01:07:12,166
which many might have forgotten.

1337
01:07:13,125 --> 01:07:16,041
You know how sometimes you want
to do something good for someone

1338
01:07:16,125 --> 01:07:18,500
and you end up doing the opposite?

1339
01:07:18,583 --> 01:07:21,416
I thought I'd put an end
to the story with the book,

1340
01:07:21,500 --> 01:07:25,291
but, in fact, I turned her into a legend.

1341
01:07:25,375 --> 01:07:27,916
MY MOTHER, YIYA MURANO

1342
01:07:28,000 --> 01:07:29,291
[dramatic music playing]

1343
01:07:29,375 --> 01:07:30,458
[keys jingling]

1344
01:07:30,541 --> 01:07:32,333
THE POISONER
WILL BE RELEASED

1345
01:07:32,416 --> 01:07:35,291
[Martín] The first thing was
that her sentence was reduced.

1346
01:07:35,375 --> 01:07:36,291
And shortly after that…

1347
01:07:36,375 --> 01:07:37,375
COMMUTING SENTENCES

1348
01:07:37,458 --> 01:07:41,791
…whoever was president at the time
gave her a presidential pardon.

1349
01:07:41,875 --> 01:07:43,208
YIYA MURANO RELEASED

1350
01:07:43,291 --> 01:07:44,875
[camera shutter clicking]

1351
01:07:47,583 --> 01:07:49,125
16 YEARS AFTER MEMA'S DEATH

1352
01:07:49,208 --> 01:07:50,916
[Martín] She got work
as soon as she was out.

1353
01:07:51,000 --> 01:07:53,208
She began selling interviews to the press.

1354
01:07:54,291 --> 01:07:56,541
She reappeared, she was pardoned,

1355
01:07:56,625 --> 01:07:58,250
and she went after the media.

1356
01:07:58,750 --> 01:08:02,000
And she was charging them money
for telling her story.

1357
01:08:02,083 --> 01:08:04,083
[applause]

1358
01:08:06,208 --> 01:08:08,166
Good afternoon. How are you, Mirtha?

1359
01:08:08,666 --> 01:08:10,166
-Good. How are you?
-You look gorgeous.

1360
01:08:10,250 --> 01:08:13,000
Thank you so much.
Come in. Are you nervous?

1361
01:08:13,083 --> 01:08:14,000
-A little bit.
-You are?

1362
01:08:14,958 --> 01:08:17,666
TV adopted Yiya as a character,
just like it adopts any monster as one.

1363
01:08:17,750 --> 01:08:18,750
REPORTER AND AUTHOR

1364
01:08:18,833 --> 01:08:22,041
Anyone who steps out of line
is someone worthy of coverage.

1365
01:08:22,125 --> 01:08:25,625
They complemented each other perfectly.
TV to Yiya, and vice versa.

1366
01:08:26,916 --> 01:08:29,833
How are you? Should I call you Yiya,
or María de las Mercedes?

1367
01:08:29,916 --> 01:08:31,416
María de las Mercedes.

1368
01:08:32,875 --> 01:08:36,500
The appeal basically came from
the mystery that Yiya Murano represented.

1369
01:08:36,583 --> 01:08:37,583
REPORTER

1370
01:08:37,666 --> 01:08:40,291
Because Yiya Murano was
and still is an enigma,

1371
01:08:40,375 --> 01:08:43,166
one I suppose
you are now attempting to decipher.

1372
01:08:43,250 --> 01:08:46,208
So, tell me,
you knew all three victims, right?

1373
01:08:46,291 --> 01:08:47,250
[Yiya] Of course I did.

1374
01:08:47,333 --> 01:08:51,333
Two were my friends, one was a relative,
a second cousin of mine.

1375
01:08:51,916 --> 01:08:53,416
I knew all three of them.

1376
01:08:54,708 --> 01:08:55,916
No, she wasn't scary.

1377
01:08:56,000 --> 01:08:58,833
You didn't get the feeling
that you were with a sinister figure,

1378
01:08:58,916 --> 01:09:00,416
even if you knew she was a murderer.

1379
01:09:00,500 --> 01:09:02,708
And when she started talking,

1380
01:09:02,791 --> 01:09:05,625
she would become very nice,
and even charming.

1381
01:09:05,708 --> 01:09:08,541
That's when you understood
how she could deceive people.

1382
01:09:08,625 --> 01:09:13,041
-You've always maintained your innocence.
-I am innocent, I'm telling you.

1383
01:09:13,125 --> 01:09:15,708
And I will repeat that
until the day I die.

1384
01:09:15,791 --> 01:09:18,750
I've never killed anyone.
I'm not a murderer.

1385
01:09:19,666 --> 01:09:23,166
I don't think she ever knew
if everything she did was true or false.

1386
01:09:23,250 --> 01:09:25,125
She was completely caught up
in her own lie.

1387
01:09:25,708 --> 01:09:27,125
She was not an easy character.

1388
01:09:30,916 --> 01:09:32,916
Interviewing a murderer is
neither easy nor pleasant.

1389
01:09:33,000 --> 01:09:33,916
REPORTER

1390
01:09:34,000 --> 01:09:35,833
They're not pleasant people
you want to talk to.

1391
01:09:35,916 --> 01:09:38,833
And they also constantly keep
denying what they did.

1392
01:09:38,916 --> 01:09:42,833
So, I found myself across from
a very severe, strong woman,

1393
01:09:42,916 --> 01:09:46,166
with lots of character, and
determined not to budge from her story.

1394
01:09:46,250 --> 01:09:51,166
We know your story through the written
press at the time of the three deaths.

1395
01:09:51,250 --> 01:09:54,916
-We also know it through your son's book.
-Yes.

1396
01:09:55,000 --> 01:09:58,833
We know that version,
which I don't think matches yours.

1397
01:09:59,333 --> 01:10:00,833
It's completely different.

1398
01:10:01,916 --> 01:10:05,750
I don't want to talk about my son,
because a mother can't badmouth her son.

1399
01:10:05,833 --> 01:10:07,916
As I told you, I've forgiven him.

1400
01:10:08,000 --> 01:10:09,500
I don't know what happened to that boy,

1401
01:10:09,583 --> 01:10:12,166
I don't know
if he's lost his mind or something.

1402
01:10:13,166 --> 01:10:16,208
I feel that Yiya went on TV
to overshadow Martín.

1403
01:10:16,291 --> 01:10:19,583
To come out and tell a different story,
and bury his story,

1404
01:10:19,666 --> 01:10:22,083
which is about her being a social climber.

1405
01:10:22,166 --> 01:10:24,916
-This book is harsh. Have you read it?
-I've read it.

1406
01:10:25,000 --> 01:10:29,166
And on the day I did, I don't know
how God kept me standing, you know?

1407
01:10:29,250 --> 01:10:30,208
Yes, I've read it.

1408
01:10:30,291 --> 01:10:34,041
I thought she was going to refute
what was written in the book,

1409
01:10:34,541 --> 01:10:37,041
not that she would attack me directly.

1410
01:10:37,125 --> 01:10:41,083
So I always wonder, what happened?
What did he want?

1411
01:10:41,166 --> 01:10:44,875
To become a writer like Borges?
I don't know, what did he really want?

1412
01:10:44,958 --> 01:10:46,958
[Martín] For me, TV is my home.

1413
01:10:47,041 --> 01:10:49,083
She picked a fight in my home.

1414
01:10:49,875 --> 01:10:51,791
I don't fall into those discussions of,

1415
01:10:51,875 --> 01:10:53,833
"They said this about you,
so I'll say that."

1416
01:10:53,916 --> 01:10:56,000
I just went there to tell the truth,

1417
01:10:56,083 --> 01:10:58,583
which is no more, no less
than what's in the book.

1418
01:10:58,666 --> 01:11:03,125
So, when you talked to her, did…
did she confess any of this to you?

1419
01:11:03,208 --> 01:11:04,083
I say it in the book.

1420
01:11:04,166 --> 01:11:06,458
-Oh, it's in the… We have to buy the book!
-[laughs]

1421
01:11:08,458 --> 01:11:11,875
Martín relates something in his book
that, when you read it, it seems like…

1422
01:11:11,958 --> 01:11:12,958
REPORTER

1423
01:11:13,041 --> 01:11:15,375
…like it's something
out of an action movie.

1424
01:11:15,458 --> 01:11:17,833
She'd been free for three years already.

1425
01:11:17,916 --> 01:11:22,791
They released her for lack of evidence,
until the court convicted her in 1985,

1426
01:11:22,875 --> 01:11:25,125
and she has to go to jail, it's done.

1427
01:11:25,208 --> 01:11:27,458
And… and she escapes.

1428
01:11:27,541 --> 01:11:29,208
[woman] Your son said you tried to escape.

1429
01:11:29,291 --> 01:11:30,541
Escape from whom?

1430
01:11:30,625 --> 01:11:33,250
-Escape your sentence, leave the country.
-Never in a million years!

1431
01:11:33,333 --> 01:11:36,083
Come on! I've never escaped.
I've always showed up in court.

1432
01:11:36,166 --> 01:11:40,208
She escaped before the…
the second conviction, right?

1433
01:11:40,708 --> 01:11:42,250
-No.
-Or she didn't show up. What was it?

1434
01:11:42,333 --> 01:11:44,666
She didn't show up
after she was convicted.

1435
01:11:44,750 --> 01:11:46,125
But did you go

1436
01:11:46,750 --> 01:11:48,291
looking for her or something?

1437
01:11:48,875 --> 01:11:50,291
I went to talk to her,

1438
01:11:51,250 --> 01:11:52,250
not to look for her.

1439
01:11:52,958 --> 01:11:55,083
She confessed to some things
about her life.

1440
01:11:55,166 --> 01:11:57,458
-And the things that had happened.
-Like what, for example?

1441
01:11:57,541 --> 01:11:59,250
That she had killed those three women.

1442
01:11:59,333 --> 01:12:00,541
-She confessed to it?
-Yes.

1443
01:12:00,625 --> 01:12:02,875
It was quite funny, really.
It's described in the book.

1444
01:12:02,958 --> 01:12:05,541
No one believes me when I tell it.
It sounds like a movie.

1445
01:12:06,416 --> 01:12:07,416
[woman] Mark.

1446
01:12:09,041 --> 01:12:11,375
[man] We're on two, and action.

1447
01:12:11,458 --> 01:12:13,458
[folk music playing]

1448
01:12:14,250 --> 01:12:16,833
[Antonio] Where will she go?
And for how long?

1449
01:12:17,416 --> 01:12:18,708
[Héctor] As long as she has to.

1450
01:12:18,791 --> 01:12:20,583
[Antonio] What's
this woman going to live on?

1451
01:12:20,666 --> 01:12:22,291
-We'll figure it out.
-[Martín] I got home.

1452
01:12:22,375 --> 01:12:23,625
[Antonio] No, no, no. I'll go…

1453
01:12:23,708 --> 01:12:25,291
[Martín] And inside my house,

1454
01:12:25,875 --> 01:12:28,958
everyone was talking at once,
asserting their opinions.

1455
01:12:29,458 --> 01:12:30,708
And when I heard

1456
01:12:31,708 --> 01:12:34,000
one of the people there,
of course, who was Cantón,

1457
01:12:34,083 --> 01:12:37,291
he was practically part of the family,
as the official lover…

1458
01:12:37,375 --> 01:12:40,250
-He said, "Well, yes…"
-She will have to go by herself.

1459
01:12:40,333 --> 01:12:42,375
[Martín] "The best thing
is for her to leave."

1460
01:12:42,458 --> 01:12:45,375
-[Héctor] Because it's easier that way.
-[Antonio] No, it's not easier.

1461
01:12:45,458 --> 01:12:47,875
[Martín] I grabbed my pack of cigarettes.

1462
01:12:47,958 --> 01:12:50,125
-I turned around and left.
-[door slams]

1463
01:12:51,000 --> 01:12:52,125
I came back

1464
01:12:52,625 --> 01:12:54,041
and Yiya was gone.

1465
01:12:54,625 --> 01:12:55,791
[ringing]

1466
01:12:55,875 --> 01:12:57,750
[Martín] That night, the phone rang.

1467
01:12:58,250 --> 01:12:59,541
I picked up.

1468
01:12:59,625 --> 01:13:02,125
-Hello?
-I was hearing a woman panting.

1469
01:13:02,208 --> 01:13:05,500
They said,
"I'm really having a great time."

1470
01:13:05,583 --> 01:13:07,625
-Who's this?
-"This is how much fun Marcela… "

1471
01:13:07,708 --> 01:13:09,708
-That was my girlfriend's name…
-Who is this?

1472
01:13:09,791 --> 01:13:11,708
"…is going to have
when my friends get her."

1473
01:13:11,791 --> 01:13:13,791
All right, why don't you go fuck yourself?

1474
01:13:15,458 --> 01:13:17,416
A while later, someone else called.

1475
01:13:17,500 --> 01:13:20,916
-[man on TV] I know that you haven't come…
-[ringing]

1476
01:13:21,958 --> 01:13:25,875
…to show support only for a government…

1477
01:13:25,958 --> 01:13:27,916
-What the fuck do you want?
-[man] Listen, kid.

1478
01:13:28,000 --> 01:13:30,500
-What?
-Your dad's a very old man.

1479
01:13:30,583 --> 01:13:33,166
-He could have a heart attack any moment.
-[TV] Democracy!

1480
01:13:34,208 --> 01:13:35,458
[man] Tell us where your mom is.

1481
01:13:35,541 --> 01:13:37,375
-Or you might regret it.
-[motorbike pulls away]

1482
01:13:37,458 --> 01:13:39,708
[man on TV] The united people
also tells them

1483
01:13:40,833 --> 01:13:43,541
that democracy is order.

1484
01:13:44,500 --> 01:13:47,500
-Dictatorship is chaos.
-[telephone ringing]

1485
01:13:54,416 --> 01:13:57,166
"Dad, where is Yiya?"

1486
01:13:57,250 --> 01:13:58,625
"I don't even know,

1487
01:13:58,708 --> 01:14:02,166
because the first person
they were going to interrogate was me."

1488
01:14:02,250 --> 01:14:06,250
[Antonio] Yes, she's doing what she can.
What's happening to her is very unfair.

1489
01:14:06,333 --> 01:14:08,583
-[Martín] Unfair!
-[car horns blaring]

1490
01:14:08,666 --> 01:14:09,791
[Martín] She deserves it all.

1491
01:14:09,875 --> 01:14:11,750
So, how did you find her?

1492
01:14:12,333 --> 01:14:14,625
Well, I went to ask someone
I thought would know.

1493
01:14:14,708 --> 01:14:16,958
He's gone now, but there's no point
in mentioning his name.

1494
01:14:17,041 --> 01:14:20,416
I asked that person where she was,
he told me, and I went to see her.

1495
01:14:20,500 --> 01:14:26,375
"She's at the house of… a certain person,
in the province of Formosa."

1496
01:14:27,583 --> 01:14:32,375
I went to the airport, and the first
person on my mind was my dad.

1497
01:14:32,458 --> 01:14:34,166
[dramatic music playing]

1498
01:14:34,791 --> 01:14:38,541
Listen, he'd get upset
because I spoke badly about Yiya.

1499
01:14:39,041 --> 01:14:40,833
If he'd known I was going to turn her in…

1500
01:14:43,000 --> 01:14:44,625
When I bought the ticket,

1501
01:14:44,708 --> 01:14:47,875
there were two guys
in suits and black glasses.

1502
01:14:47,958 --> 01:14:49,541
They were staring at me.

1503
01:14:49,625 --> 01:14:52,916
And then they sat two rows
behind me on the plane.

1504
01:15:03,125 --> 01:15:06,833
We got off, I asked someone
where that neighborhood was…

1505
01:15:06,916 --> 01:15:08,458
[birdsong]

1506
01:15:08,541 --> 01:15:10,041
[suspenseful music playing]

1507
01:15:10,958 --> 01:15:12,625
And it felt like a comedy act.

1508
01:15:12,708 --> 01:15:18,208
I walked a few meters, I'd stop,
and the guys also stopped.

1509
01:15:22,000 --> 01:15:24,583
I said, "Get ready to walk,
it's a good 20 blocks still,

1510
01:15:24,666 --> 01:15:27,708
and you'll be sweating like crazy
in those jackets."

1511
01:15:29,833 --> 01:15:31,125
They just ignored me.

1512
01:15:35,750 --> 01:15:38,875
The humidity was high,
and the sun was blazing.

1513
01:15:49,416 --> 01:15:53,416
At that time, as far as I know, that stuff
about men in black following you

1514
01:15:53,500 --> 01:15:57,458
is a bit like something
you see in a movie.

1515
01:15:58,416 --> 01:16:00,458
It sounds like they were
intelligence services.

1516
01:16:00,541 --> 01:16:02,000
The why isn't explained

1517
01:16:02,083 --> 01:16:05,458
but it's not unreasonable to think
that in the search for a fugitive,

1518
01:16:05,541 --> 01:16:10,250
there might be a surveillance operation
targeting their circle.

1519
01:16:11,291 --> 01:16:12,833
[chicken clucks]

1520
01:16:21,000 --> 01:16:23,083
[rooster crows]

1521
01:16:25,458 --> 01:16:27,458
[suspenseful music playing]

1522
01:16:29,416 --> 01:16:32,208
[Martín] I got there, where Yiya was.

1523
01:16:48,875 --> 01:16:50,666
And she said, "What are you doing here?"

1524
01:16:51,416 --> 01:16:52,666
"I'm here for you."

1525
01:16:54,625 --> 01:16:56,208
"You've got nothing left to lose."

1526
01:16:57,250 --> 01:17:01,458
"Why don't you try telling the truth for
once in your life and see how it feels?"

1527
01:17:01,541 --> 01:17:04,375
-Tell me, how did you do it?
-[Yiya] Do what?

1528
01:17:05,125 --> 01:17:06,000
Kill them.

1529
01:17:07,791 --> 01:17:09,041
Where was the poison?

1530
01:17:09,958 --> 01:17:11,458
Why does it even matter?

1531
01:17:12,625 --> 01:17:13,750
Martín…

1532
01:17:13,833 --> 01:17:16,333
[producer] How did she admit that?
"Yes, I killed them."

1533
01:17:16,416 --> 01:17:18,708
Because she had never
owned up to it before.

1534
01:17:18,791 --> 01:17:20,625
Exactly how you're telling me.

1535
01:17:22,000 --> 01:17:23,166
[chuckling]

1536
01:17:24,083 --> 01:17:26,708
Well… what Martín says

1537
01:17:26,791 --> 01:17:28,333
is a complete fantasy.

1538
01:17:28,416 --> 01:17:30,625
She denied it her entire life,

1539
01:17:31,166 --> 01:17:35,041
and it was deeply ingrained in her
that she had not done it.

1540
01:17:35,125 --> 01:17:38,708
If I could peek inside her brain,
I'd say, "Oh, it wasn't her."

1541
01:17:38,791 --> 01:17:41,583
Because she truly was convinced
she had nothing to do with it.

1542
01:17:41,666 --> 01:17:44,583
She couldn't confess to something
she didn't believe she did.

1543
01:17:45,666 --> 01:17:47,708
If she'd wanted to put him at ease,
she would've said,

1544
01:17:47,791 --> 01:17:49,500
"I'm innocent. I'm incapable of killing."

1545
01:17:49,583 --> 01:17:52,125
If she'd wanted to torture him,
she'd tell him all her crimes.

1546
01:17:52,208 --> 01:17:56,250
Thinking about the way that mother
operated, wanting to destroy her son…

1547
01:17:56,333 --> 01:18:00,166
And to make matters even worse,
he ends up being a snitch.

1548
01:18:00,666 --> 01:18:02,083
He turns his mother in.

1549
01:18:02,583 --> 01:18:05,791
Maybe, it could be
that Yiya, to be vengeful,

1550
01:18:05,875 --> 01:18:07,291
ended up telling him everything.

1551
01:18:07,375 --> 01:18:09,791
I've never confessed
to my son or anyone else.

1552
01:18:09,875 --> 01:18:11,958
-I will swear on my son's life.
-[woman] I see.

1553
01:18:12,041 --> 01:18:14,916
Who I deeply love,
although he has tarnished me.

1554
01:18:15,000 --> 01:18:16,416
I've never confessed anything.

1555
01:18:17,500 --> 01:18:21,291
She told truths mixed with lies.
And with some half-truths.

1556
01:18:21,375 --> 01:18:23,458
Because she had nothing left to lose.

1557
01:18:23,541 --> 01:18:27,041
And all this circus, all this shit,
it's not good for me or my dad.

1558
01:18:29,791 --> 01:18:31,041
He's not your dad.

1559
01:18:33,833 --> 01:18:36,375
What? You're surprised?

1560
01:18:38,791 --> 01:18:40,291
Didn't you want to know?

1561
01:18:43,708 --> 01:18:45,541
Antonio's not your father.

1562
01:19:04,458 --> 01:19:09,208
I suspected that he wasn't my dad,
but, you know, I never cared.

1563
01:19:13,958 --> 01:19:15,291
I never cared.

1564
01:19:17,416 --> 01:19:21,000
The question of his father's identity
eventually wore Martín down.

1565
01:19:21,083 --> 01:19:24,250
That's why I believe Martín was,
in a way, her very first victim.

1566
01:19:25,916 --> 01:19:29,333
Personally, I don't believe that she had
confessed to her son about her crimes.

1567
01:19:29,416 --> 01:19:33,166
But if she wanted to hurt him,
she probably told him about his father.

1568
01:19:33,666 --> 01:19:35,708
Because telling him
about his father does hurt him.

1569
01:19:35,791 --> 01:19:39,375
Martín said that you would say
that your husband was infertile.

1570
01:19:39,500 --> 01:19:42,250
-How could I say that?
-And that you didn't want to have a child.

1571
01:19:42,333 --> 01:19:44,833
Oh, please, that's awful. Good Lord.

1572
01:19:46,166 --> 01:19:47,041
Anyway.

1573
01:19:47,125 --> 01:19:51,041
-Was your husband fertile?
-Please. He's Antonio Murano's son.

1574
01:19:51,125 --> 01:19:54,333
He's built like Antonio Murano,
but he's got my face.

1575
01:19:54,416 --> 01:19:57,083
I apologize for that,
because I'm not very attractive.

1576
01:19:57,166 --> 01:19:59,958
In that sense, yes.
He looks a lot like me.

1577
01:20:01,000 --> 01:20:03,250
Her son is a separate chapter
in this story,

1578
01:20:03,333 --> 01:20:05,750
because he's very similar to his mother.

1579
01:20:05,833 --> 01:20:07,500
I mean, he's not a murderer.

1580
01:20:07,583 --> 01:20:11,625
I think he has the advantage
of not being an evil person.

1581
01:20:11,708 --> 01:20:16,625
But he once spread the rumor
that he was dead.

1582
01:20:17,250 --> 01:20:19,500
I called him, he answered.

1583
01:20:20,333 --> 01:20:24,041
I said, "Weren't you dead?"
"No, I spread the rumor myself."

1584
01:20:24,750 --> 01:20:27,166
I mean, like mother, like son, you know?

1585
01:20:28,458 --> 01:20:30,458
It's very clear that Martín also inherited

1586
01:20:30,541 --> 01:20:33,375
some of that desire
for fame and attention she had.

1587
01:20:33,458 --> 01:20:37,666
His mom wanted to be famous,
and he saw an open door to make it happen.

1588
01:20:39,041 --> 01:20:42,166
It's better to inherit that,
than killing his friends.

1589
01:20:43,333 --> 01:20:46,291
It must be very difficult
to be Yiya Murano's son.

1590
01:20:46,375 --> 01:20:48,500
I think it must be extremely hard

1591
01:20:48,583 --> 01:20:51,750
to stand in front of society
and say, "I'm the son of a murderer."

1592
01:20:51,833 --> 01:20:54,375
I'm glad to know
that you were able to rebuild your life

1593
01:20:54,458 --> 01:20:59,166
after something so… so hard,
so… so sinister, like your experience.

1594
01:20:59,250 --> 01:21:03,041
-Thank you.
-You've done quite a few dangerous jobs.

1595
01:21:03,125 --> 01:21:06,250
Well, they included
jumping off of a balcony,

1596
01:21:06,333 --> 01:21:08,458
doing car scenes, car crashes…

1597
01:21:08,541 --> 01:21:10,083
-I see.
-Fire, stuff like that.

1598
01:21:10,166 --> 01:21:14,125
Look, I'm not a psychologist, so I'm not
going to call that your suicidal side.

1599
01:21:14,708 --> 01:21:16,375
[muffled voices]

1600
01:21:17,625 --> 01:21:19,708
[Martín] This is a robbery!
Everybody freeze!

1601
01:21:19,791 --> 01:21:20,666
[all gasping]

1602
01:21:21,458 --> 01:21:23,041
Give me the bag.

1603
01:21:23,125 --> 01:21:24,166
[screaming]

1604
01:21:24,250 --> 01:21:26,458
Against the wall, come on.
Against the wall!

1605
01:21:26,541 --> 01:21:27,958
[woman] Please, no. Please!

1606
01:21:30,000 --> 01:21:33,250
Beyond what we know how to do,
we know nothing else.

1607
01:21:33,333 --> 01:21:35,125
CHANNEL 9 SALE
TO AUSTRALIAN NETWORK CONFIRMED

1608
01:21:35,208 --> 01:21:36,166
When Channel 9 closed…

1609
01:21:36,250 --> 01:21:37,625
ARGENTINE ASSOCIATION OF ACTORS

1610
01:21:37,708 --> 01:21:39,708
…there were thousands
of us actors out of work.

1611
01:21:39,791 --> 01:21:41,000
WE WANT TO WORK

1612
01:21:41,083 --> 01:21:43,708
And the only thing I can do,
modestly speaking,

1613
01:21:43,791 --> 01:21:47,416
I'm not even sure I really know how,
but I could try to,

1614
01:21:47,500 --> 01:21:49,250
is something to do with media.

1615
01:21:49,333 --> 01:21:50,625
I didn't know what to do.

1616
01:21:50,708 --> 01:21:51,875
VACANT

1617
01:21:51,958 --> 01:21:55,833
[Martín] And one of the producers
had a couple of taxis.

1618
01:21:56,333 --> 01:22:00,166
And he told me, "You're going through
a hard time right now, aren't you?"

1619
01:22:00,250 --> 01:22:05,000
"Well, here's what we're gonna do.
I'll give you a taxi for two weeks."

1620
01:22:05,583 --> 01:22:06,666
"No need to pay me."

1621
01:22:08,583 --> 01:22:11,000
He'd give me the taxi at night.

1622
01:22:13,125 --> 01:22:15,166
The night is not only different

1623
01:22:15,250 --> 01:22:18,250
because of the visual clarity
and darkness of the lights,

1624
01:22:19,208 --> 01:22:24,125
but also in the clarity
and darkness within people, spiritually.

1625
01:22:24,208 --> 01:22:25,791
[wistful music playing]

1626
01:22:26,500 --> 01:22:29,125
[Martín] I would eat
two hard-boiled eggs a day.

1627
01:22:30,083 --> 01:22:32,041
The market vendor gave them to me.

1628
01:22:33,250 --> 01:22:35,458
And that's when I realized that…

1629
01:22:37,000 --> 01:22:38,541
that I had no friends.

1630
01:22:39,416 --> 01:22:41,041
That I had no family.

1631
01:22:41,750 --> 01:22:44,500
Okay, "Yiya's son,
merciless Martín Murano."

1632
01:22:44,583 --> 01:22:47,750
"As a child, I began to feel
repulsion toward my mother."

1633
01:22:48,416 --> 01:22:50,625
Have you seen your son again, Yiya?

1634
01:22:50,708 --> 01:22:54,041
Mirtha, my son called me a year ago.

1635
01:22:54,125 --> 01:22:56,500
He said, "Mother, I do understand…"

1636
01:22:56,583 --> 01:22:59,041
No, he didn't say Mother, he said Yiya.

1637
01:22:59,125 --> 01:23:02,125
"I understand," he said,
"that I've done you great harm."

1638
01:23:02,208 --> 01:23:03,958
And he admitted he'd been wrong.

1639
01:23:04,458 --> 01:23:07,791
-I believe we'll patch things up soon.
-[Mirtha] Really?

1640
01:23:09,125 --> 01:23:10,958
I mean, I had lost my…

1641
01:23:11,916 --> 01:23:14,125
my dignity, my self-esteem.

1642
01:23:14,708 --> 01:23:19,041
I looked at the pictures in my kitchen,
jumping off buildings and stuff,

1643
01:23:19,125 --> 01:23:23,083
and I said, "That's so long ago,
it seems like it wasn't me."

1644
01:23:24,375 --> 01:23:29,416
Let's now welcome Mr. Martín Murano,
who's the son of Mrs. Yiya Murano.

1645
01:23:29,500 --> 01:23:31,958
-[applause]
-Come on in, Martín. How are you?

1646
01:23:32,458 --> 01:23:36,291
[Martín] Well, we went on that famous
TV show where guests would have lunch.

1647
01:23:36,375 --> 01:23:40,375
And the host came in saying
that we were there that day to celebrate…

1648
01:23:40,458 --> 01:23:42,208
-Are you going to say hi to your mother?
-Yes.

1649
01:23:42,291 --> 01:23:44,208
…our mother-son reconciliation.

1650
01:23:44,875 --> 01:23:47,000
[Mirtha] Where have you been?
You disappeared.

1651
01:23:47,083 --> 01:23:48,958
-[laughs]
-We called the boarding house.

1652
01:23:49,041 --> 01:23:50,541
They told us you weren't there anymore.

1653
01:23:50,625 --> 01:23:52,833
It was very hard to find you,
Martín, very hard.

1654
01:23:52,916 --> 01:23:56,875
I want to thank my crew,
who spent over a week looking for you.

1655
01:23:56,958 --> 01:24:00,041
Well, actually, I'd rather
we talked about a reunion today.

1656
01:24:00,125 --> 01:24:01,666
-If that's okay.
-[Mirtha] With your son?

1657
01:24:01,750 --> 01:24:04,333
-Yes, it really is something, isn't it?
-[Yiya] Oh, yes.

1658
01:24:04,416 --> 01:24:07,125
-It sure is something.
-[Mirtha] You were on bad terms, right?

1659
01:24:07,625 --> 01:24:11,250
Mirtha, let's say we were neither
on bad terms nor on good terms.

1660
01:24:11,333 --> 01:24:14,000
We didn't see each other for seven years.

1661
01:24:14,500 --> 01:24:16,833
[Mirtha] So how did you
make up with your mom?

1662
01:24:16,916 --> 01:24:19,625
[Martín] I never had
a relationship with Yiya Murano.

1663
01:24:19,708 --> 01:24:22,583
So you can't fix something
that just never existed.

1664
01:24:23,208 --> 01:24:25,583
We can debate all we want,
we can talk for hours,

1665
01:24:25,666 --> 01:24:28,458
but you still are what you are…
a murderer.

1666
01:24:28,541 --> 01:24:31,666
I… I don't know if…
if we can talk about making up.

1667
01:24:31,750 --> 01:24:34,625
I think the ideal thing would be to

1668
01:24:35,166 --> 01:24:37,833
leave things behind sometimes, right?

1669
01:24:37,916 --> 01:24:41,125
And try to start over from scratch,

1670
01:24:41,208 --> 01:24:44,291
to… to see what can be done
moving forward, right?

1671
01:24:45,458 --> 01:24:47,625
And she said, "My son and I are deciding

1672
01:24:47,708 --> 01:24:49,958
whether to write a book
telling the truth."

1673
01:24:50,041 --> 01:24:52,208
[Yiya] I've almost finished the book
at this point.

1674
01:24:52,291 --> 01:24:53,416
-[Mirtha] Really?
-Yes.

1675
01:24:53,500 --> 01:24:56,791
So, I'm going to ask my son
if I should publish it or not.

1676
01:24:57,500 --> 01:25:00,166
[Martín] Obviously, I made a face like,
"What are you talking about?"

1677
01:25:00,250 --> 01:25:02,791
Out of respect for the show,

1678
01:25:02,875 --> 01:25:04,541
for the TV host,

1679
01:25:05,041 --> 01:25:06,583
and for myself,

1680
01:25:07,416 --> 01:25:10,000
I didn't answer
what I should have answered.

1681
01:25:10,083 --> 01:25:12,541
But I really had a bad time on that show.

1682
01:25:12,625 --> 01:25:15,875
Your mother's in front of you, Martín.
Do you want to say something to her?

1683
01:25:15,958 --> 01:25:17,625
-No? Nothing in particular?
-No, no, no.

1684
01:25:18,291 --> 01:25:21,291
[Martín] That's when I said to myself,
"Who do you think you were?"

1685
01:25:22,375 --> 01:25:25,833
"Did you think you were someone
important on TV? You're not."

1686
01:25:29,666 --> 01:25:31,250
"And whatever you do,

1687
01:25:31,750 --> 01:25:34,208
you'll always be Yiya Murano's son."

1688
01:25:34,291 --> 01:25:36,166
"You'll never have a life of your own."

1689
01:25:41,791 --> 01:25:45,458
And that's true. He ended up
perpetuating what he wanted to kill,

1690
01:25:45,541 --> 01:25:47,125
which was his mother's memory.

1691
01:25:47,208 --> 01:25:50,333
Because he wrote the book as catharsis.

1692
01:25:50,416 --> 01:25:53,375
It may have been catharsis,
but he also summoned the monster.

1693
01:25:53,458 --> 01:25:56,833
What's going on in your life, Yiya?
You've killed a lot of people, Yiya.

1694
01:25:56,916 --> 01:25:59,791
Did you poison them?
Is that it? What did you do, Yiya?

1695
01:25:59,875 --> 01:26:03,208
I swear to God and on the Bible
that I didn't kill anyone.

1696
01:26:03,791 --> 01:26:05,791
She also started creating
her own character.

1697
01:26:05,875 --> 01:26:08,125
She gave interviews
and said she was innocent,

1698
01:26:08,208 --> 01:26:10,708
but her constant attitude
was that of a guilty person.

1699
01:26:10,791 --> 01:26:12,458
Because she liked to play with that.

1700
01:26:12,541 --> 01:26:15,625
You could torture me
and I'd still swear it's true,

1701
01:26:15,708 --> 01:26:17,416
I've never killed anyone.

1702
01:26:17,500 --> 01:26:20,083
-[man] Swear on your life?
-I swear on my son's life.

1703
01:26:20,166 --> 01:26:21,541
-[man] Don't joke about that.
-I'm--

1704
01:26:21,625 --> 01:26:22,791
[man] Are you being serious?

1705
01:26:22,875 --> 01:26:26,125
It was all because
of her ego and her narcissism.

1706
01:26:26,208 --> 01:26:28,083
It was just more powerful
than anything else.

1707
01:26:28,875 --> 01:26:31,500
I remember she was
on one of those shows late at night.

1708
01:26:31,583 --> 01:26:35,333
They hired her to just talk about life.

1709
01:26:35,416 --> 01:26:37,708
Hello, friends,
I'm here with you once again,

1710
01:26:37,791 --> 01:26:40,041
like I am every Wednesday at this time,

1711
01:26:40,125 --> 01:26:42,875
to try to solve the problems
and all the questions

1712
01:26:42,958 --> 01:26:45,500
that you keep sending me
by letter or by phone,

1713
01:26:45,583 --> 01:26:46,833
and that I receive daily.

1714
01:26:46,916 --> 01:26:48,833
It reinforced that character

1715
01:26:48,916 --> 01:26:51,041
that keeps us talking
about Yiya even today,

1716
01:26:51,125 --> 01:26:52,375
and that will endure for years.

1717
01:26:52,458 --> 01:26:54,708
Oh, that I did.
Sorry, I killed two people.

1718
01:26:54,791 --> 01:26:56,458
My mother and my husband first.

1719
01:26:56,541 --> 01:26:59,291
They died of sadness
due to what happened to me.

1720
01:26:59,375 --> 01:27:01,541
I met her in one of the most bizarre ways.

1721
01:27:01,625 --> 01:27:05,458
I had written a story about Yiya
in a column called "Crime Stories,"

1722
01:27:05,541 --> 01:27:07,208
and I wrote about Yiya Murano.

1723
01:27:07,291 --> 01:27:08,250
POISON AT TEA TIME

1724
01:27:08,333 --> 01:27:10,416
[Rodolfo] I was in the newsroom
on the ninth floor,

1725
01:27:10,500 --> 01:27:11,791
and when I went downstairs,

1726
01:27:11,875 --> 01:27:15,083
I saw Yiya in a coat,
sitting in the lobby.

1727
01:27:15,166 --> 01:27:18,750
And she said to me, "Rodolfo,
you naughty boy, you wrote about me."

1728
01:27:18,833 --> 01:27:20,791
"I want the right to respond," she said.

1729
01:27:20,875 --> 01:27:23,583
She was wearing makeup, glasses,
perfectly put together.

1730
01:27:23,666 --> 01:27:26,291
"I came from Mar del Plata,
that's 404 kilometers."

1731
01:27:26,375 --> 01:27:28,333
"The taxi's waiting outside."

1732
01:27:28,416 --> 01:27:31,208
"If you pay for the round trip
and the interview, I'll do it."

1733
01:27:31,291 --> 01:27:32,958
"Sorry, that's impossible," I told her.

1734
01:27:33,041 --> 01:27:36,083
"I came all this way just for this. Who's
going to pay for the round trip now?"

1735
01:27:36,166 --> 01:27:38,875
The photographer and I
went out to spy on her.

1736
01:27:38,958 --> 01:27:40,875
And she just walked away.
There was no taxi.

1737
01:27:40,958 --> 01:27:42,083
[man whistles]

1738
01:27:44,500 --> 01:27:46,958
Yiya was someone
who showed up in newsrooms.

1739
01:27:47,041 --> 01:27:49,833
I remember seeing her there
with her little handbag,

1740
01:27:49,916 --> 01:27:52,583
and she wanted money to give interviews.

1741
01:27:52,666 --> 01:27:56,541
The truth is, interviews are not paid for,
so she would leave kind of bewildered.

1742
01:27:56,625 --> 01:27:57,666
[ringing tone]

1743
01:27:58,416 --> 01:28:01,416
[Rodolfo] She called the next day,
and said, "Let's do the interview."

1744
01:28:01,500 --> 01:28:02,708
"I'm not going to charge you,

1745
01:28:02,791 --> 01:28:05,916
but you'll invite me out to lunch
somewhere or to have tea."

1746
01:28:07,375 --> 01:28:09,541
So we met her at Las Violetas.

1747
01:28:10,375 --> 01:28:13,708
She ordered pastries,
she ordered some specialty cookies,

1748
01:28:14,291 --> 01:28:15,958
and little finger sandwiches.

1749
01:28:16,041 --> 01:28:18,083
She had tea, she had coffee.

1750
01:28:18,166 --> 01:28:19,875
She had a voracious appetite.

1751
01:28:23,000 --> 01:28:25,708
"THIS COUNTRY IS A FACTORY OF MURDERERS"

1752
01:28:26,250 --> 01:28:27,333
Yiya wanted to be famous.

1753
01:28:27,416 --> 01:28:30,708
Once she saw the cameras on her,

1754
01:28:31,333 --> 01:28:32,583
she said, "I'm not leaving."

1755
01:28:32,666 --> 01:28:35,750
And television allowed it,
and society allowed it too.

1756
01:28:36,333 --> 01:28:39,833
Well, she put on that costume
of already being a crime legend…

1757
01:28:39,916 --> 01:28:41,916
[romantic music playing]

1758
01:28:43,166 --> 01:28:44,416
[Rodolfo] …and went for it.

1759
01:28:46,750 --> 01:28:48,416
She faced her destiny.

1760
01:28:50,583 --> 01:28:53,125
[woman] Mirtha Legrand.

1761
01:28:53,625 --> 01:28:56,000
[Rodolfo] Maybe that's where
she found who she was.

1762
01:28:56,083 --> 01:28:58,791
[woman] Mrs. Yiya Murano.

1763
01:28:59,291 --> 01:29:03,291
She was in jail,
accused of poisoning her friends.

1764
01:29:03,375 --> 01:29:05,083
[Rodolfo] It didn't matter
that on that show,

1765
01:29:05,166 --> 01:29:06,666
they introduced her that way.

1766
01:29:06,750 --> 01:29:09,916
"For lunch, joining Mirtha Legrand,
Yiya Murano, the Poisoner."

1767
01:29:10,000 --> 01:29:11,041
She didn't care about that.

1768
01:29:11,125 --> 01:29:13,291
I think it had to do with
her own perversion.

1769
01:29:13,375 --> 01:29:15,666
She actually enjoyed being
"The Poisoner of Monserrat."

1770
01:29:16,375 --> 01:29:19,000
It's like a role, or like a job, you know?
That's very odd.

1771
01:29:19,083 --> 01:29:20,791
I haven't seen that in any other case.

1772
01:29:20,875 --> 01:29:22,666
[Mirtha] Does it bother you
when they call you…

1773
01:29:22,750 --> 01:29:26,791
I'll cover up because I'm embarrassed.
"The Poisoner of Monserrat."

1774
01:29:27,291 --> 01:29:29,166
-It doesn't bother me.
-[Mirtha] Does it?

1775
01:29:29,250 --> 01:29:32,083
Because I know what I did
and what I didn't do.

1776
01:29:32,166 --> 01:29:33,916
So today I can tell you, Mirtha.

1777
01:29:34,000 --> 01:29:36,333
-It doesn't bother me at all.
-[Mirtha] It doesn't.

1778
01:29:36,416 --> 01:29:38,875
Over time, Yiya became a phenomenon,

1779
01:29:38,958 --> 01:29:41,958
culturally,
and even in the entertainment world.

1780
01:29:42,041 --> 01:29:45,125
When she goes to Mirtha Legrand's
show and brings pastries,

1781
01:29:45,208 --> 01:29:46,541
she becomes something else.

1782
01:29:46,625 --> 01:29:48,750
What did you bring? You brought pastries?

1783
01:29:48,833 --> 01:29:50,916
I brought pastries on one condition.

1784
01:29:51,000 --> 01:29:53,750
-That you have one.
-[Mirtha] Did you touch them?

1785
01:29:53,833 --> 01:29:54,916
-[laughter]
-No.

1786
01:29:55,500 --> 01:29:58,166
-I didn't even… Look. Hands up.
-[man] Should we call the DA?

1787
01:29:58,250 --> 01:29:59,666
-I'll have a pastry.
-[woman] Not me.

1788
01:29:59,750 --> 01:30:01,666
I'm going to have one of these pastries.

1789
01:30:01,750 --> 01:30:04,125
Now, if I don't come back tomorrow…

1790
01:30:05,208 --> 01:30:08,041
[Lía] I think she probably
was a great admirer of Mirtha,

1791
01:30:08,125 --> 01:30:10,166
because Mirtha was a beautiful woman.

1792
01:30:10,250 --> 01:30:13,958
She was the ultimate star.
And everyone watched her show.

1793
01:30:15,041 --> 01:30:18,333
You see them together at that moment,
and you'd think they connected.

1794
01:30:18,916 --> 01:30:21,125
Yiya was in her element,
and so was Mirtha.

1795
01:30:21,875 --> 01:30:25,291
In a way, Mirtha let her play,
but Mirtha is a very smart woman.

1796
01:30:25,375 --> 01:30:27,416
If she let her play,
it was because it suited her.

1797
01:30:27,500 --> 01:30:28,666
If not, she wouldn't have.

1798
01:30:28,750 --> 01:30:31,833
[Mirtha] This will be the one.
You're my witnesses, okay?

1799
01:30:31,916 --> 01:30:33,916
[guests laughing]

1800
01:30:35,250 --> 01:30:36,375
[woman] She liked it.

1801
01:30:37,708 --> 01:30:38,958
See? And you liked it.

1802
01:30:39,041 --> 01:30:42,041
-I liked it. If I don't call you tomorrow…
[Yiya] I'm glad.

1803
01:30:42,125 --> 01:30:44,666
You know why. Tomorrow's breaking news!

1804
01:30:44,750 --> 01:30:46,291
[Yiya laughs]

1805
01:30:46,375 --> 01:30:49,541
We'll be back after the break.
I'm glad we can laugh about it.

1806
01:30:50,041 --> 01:30:53,125
I think Yiya was the biggest
pop criminal in Argentina.

1807
01:30:53,625 --> 01:30:56,541
Like, Andy Warhol would've
done those things of Yiya.

1808
01:30:56,625 --> 01:30:58,125
She's an iconic image.

1809
01:30:58,208 --> 01:31:00,208
She became an iconic image

1810
01:31:00,708 --> 01:31:04,541
of evil and, at the same time,
of something kitsch, of something comedic.

1811
01:31:05,333 --> 01:31:09,291
There's something very weird
about accepting these monsters,

1812
01:31:09,375 --> 01:31:11,375
without going through
a process of repentance,

1813
01:31:11,458 --> 01:31:13,166
or recognition, or forgiveness.

1814
01:31:13,250 --> 01:31:14,458
That part seems to be missing.

1815
01:31:15,500 --> 01:31:19,833
She's a fascinating character. I think
that that's what defines her, fascination.

1816
01:31:19,916 --> 01:31:21,750
Fascination isn't necessarily moral.

1817
01:31:22,666 --> 01:31:25,708
I remember asking her once, "Yiya,
how would you like to be remembered?"

1818
01:31:25,791 --> 01:31:27,208
"With a smile," she said.

1819
01:31:27,291 --> 01:31:28,625
-[laughter]
-[soft jazz playing]

1820
01:31:28,708 --> 01:31:31,041
[Ozvaldo] When we came up with
the idea of doing the musical,

1821
01:31:31,125 --> 01:31:34,458
I was interested in the process
of figuring out

1822
01:31:34,541 --> 01:31:38,166
how a monstrous person
turns into a figure that's picturesque.

1823
01:31:38,250 --> 01:31:40,833
Anyone in the room
wanna try my cream puffs?

1824
01:31:40,916 --> 01:31:42,916
[laughter]

1825
01:31:43,000 --> 01:31:47,083
Oh, you have no idea.
They're delicious. They're to die for.

1826
01:31:48,083 --> 01:31:49,750
[Rodolfo] It's weird. Everything about it.

1827
01:31:49,833 --> 01:31:51,791
-The main character's a murderer.
-They are killer!

1828
01:31:51,875 --> 01:31:53,166
A musical…

1829
01:31:53,250 --> 01:31:55,833
But it kind of symbolizes
what this case was.

1830
01:31:56,666 --> 01:31:59,333
It was crazy when we found out
that she used to come

1831
01:31:59,416 --> 01:32:02,416
to the exact same theater
where we were doing the play.

1832
01:32:02,500 --> 01:32:06,250
Knowing that people walked
on the same sidewalk where she had stood,

1833
01:32:06,333 --> 01:32:09,708
that they might sit
in the same seat she had sat in,

1834
01:32:09,791 --> 01:32:11,916
it was magical, like a full circle moment.

1835
01:32:12,708 --> 01:32:14,708
That's exactly what Yiya would've wanted.

1836
01:32:14,791 --> 01:32:16,583
[producer] Did you like the musical?

1837
01:32:16,666 --> 01:32:18,541
I saw it ten times.

1838
01:32:18,625 --> 01:32:20,750
I don't know if that
answers your question.

1839
01:32:21,166 --> 01:32:23,916
UP FOR AUCTION: THE TEA SET
YIYA MURANO USED TO POISON HER FRIENDS

1840
01:32:24,000 --> 01:32:27,208
She signed autographs, took photos,
let people take photos of her.

1841
01:32:27,875 --> 01:32:28,833
They made a doll of her.

1842
01:32:28,916 --> 01:32:30,583
YIYA MURANO
THE POISONER OF MONSERRAT

1843
01:32:31,708 --> 01:32:34,458
They made T-shirts of her,
with her quotes, with the pastries.

1844
01:32:34,541 --> 01:32:35,583
BLACK T-SHIRT

1845
01:32:35,666 --> 01:32:37,000
HAPPY FRIENDSHIP DAY!
I'LL MISS YOU

1846
01:32:37,083 --> 01:32:39,875
It started to be widely used
in popular culture.

1847
01:32:39,958 --> 01:32:43,375
You saw a tray of pastries,
you'd say, "These are Yiya's."

1848
01:32:43,458 --> 01:32:45,750
She became part of the popular humor.

1849
01:32:46,333 --> 01:32:47,583
A SPOT OF TEA FOR FRIENDSHIP DAY

1850
01:32:47,666 --> 01:32:49,833
[Rodolfo] The jokes are still
making rounds to this day.

1851
01:32:49,916 --> 01:32:51,625
"Oh, this was brought by Yiya Murano!"

1852
01:32:51,708 --> 01:32:54,708
Or "Yiya sent it to you."
I've heard it everywhere.

1853
01:32:54,791 --> 01:32:55,666
HAPPY FRIENDSHIP DAY

1854
01:32:55,750 --> 01:32:58,333
Sometimes my colleagues,
who know I worked the case,

1855
01:32:58,416 --> 01:33:01,958
send me pictures of Yiya with pastries.

1856
01:33:02,041 --> 01:33:05,500
ON SWEETNESS WEEK, HERE ARE
SOME PASTRIES I MADE FOR YOU WITH LOVE

1857
01:33:05,583 --> 01:33:09,583
Yeah, I've heard it. In fact, even I can
joke about it with you. [chuckles]

1858
01:33:09,666 --> 01:33:11,833
"Here, have some tea, it's poison-free."

1859
01:33:13,166 --> 01:33:14,750
Sometimes it bothers me,

1860
01:33:15,250 --> 01:33:17,708
and other times, I can see the humor,

1861
01:33:17,791 --> 01:33:19,583
because I'm able
to step outside the story.

1862
01:33:19,666 --> 01:33:22,666
If you're on the outside, it's funny.
If you're on the inside, it's not.

1863
01:33:23,416 --> 01:33:24,458
Mmm…

1864
01:33:25,000 --> 01:33:27,375
I don't find it funny,
I think it's outrageous.

1865
01:33:27,458 --> 01:33:31,291
We have this ability in Argentina
to turn any tragedy into a joke,

1866
01:33:31,375 --> 01:33:32,833
something to laugh about.

1867
01:33:33,375 --> 01:33:37,833
And mixed in with all of that, sometimes
these things, these issues come up.

1868
01:33:38,708 --> 01:33:42,416
Turning someone into a funny character,
when… when you think about it,

1869
01:33:42,500 --> 01:33:44,333
if you remembered them the way you should,

1870
01:33:44,416 --> 01:33:46,666
you'd say "Hey, this was very wrong,"

1871
01:33:46,750 --> 01:33:48,916
not, "Oh, wow, that's hilarious."

1872
01:33:49,625 --> 01:33:53,000
Yiya's seen as a form of entertainment.
So people think,

1873
01:33:53,083 --> 01:33:55,416
"Ha! Look at what she did.
Can you believe it?"

1874
01:33:55,500 --> 01:33:57,208
"What a bitch." That's it.

1875
01:33:57,791 --> 01:33:59,666
No one thinks about the victims.

1876
01:33:59,750 --> 01:34:01,750
[poignant music playing]

1877
01:34:26,291 --> 01:34:30,583
Sorry, I get a bit emotional,
but my grandma was lots of things.

1878
01:34:31,958 --> 01:34:36,500
And to see her reduced to nothing
more than Yiya's last victim,

1879
01:34:37,041 --> 01:34:38,416
it's just too much.

1880
01:34:38,916 --> 01:34:41,458
This is my grandma with my mom, as a baby.

1881
01:34:41,541 --> 01:34:43,583
Because my grandma
was a beautiful person.

1882
01:34:43,666 --> 01:34:44,875
This is my grandma with my mom…

1883
01:34:44,958 --> 01:34:48,666
Strong, very loved, who did so much good.

1884
01:34:49,250 --> 01:34:51,750
To be reduced to that is terrible.

1885
01:34:53,416 --> 01:34:55,625
Here, where my finger is, that's my mom.

1886
01:34:57,000 --> 01:35:00,875
It was '77, she died in '79.
It was two years before she died.

1887
01:35:01,458 --> 01:35:05,333
Nilda's here, that's her.
Here's my daughter, and this is my son.

1888
01:35:05,416 --> 01:35:09,583
It was my daughter's first birthday,
a few months before she passed.

1889
01:35:17,000 --> 01:35:18,916
[Mariana] And then,
if on top of everything,

1890
01:35:19,000 --> 01:35:22,375
you add the fact
that the murderer became a public figure,

1891
01:35:23,375 --> 01:35:26,750
that really makes me feel
justice was not done.

1892
01:35:46,500 --> 01:35:51,916
If the memory of these people is revived,
then yes, I think justice is done.

1893
01:35:52,000 --> 01:35:55,041
Because they matter more than Yiya.

1894
01:35:56,291 --> 01:35:58,875
I'd like to tell you
that I was just thinking

1895
01:35:58,958 --> 01:36:04,291
about the families of these people
who passed away, supposedly,

1896
01:36:04,375 --> 01:36:09,500
well, because of
the actions taken by Yiya Murano.

1897
01:36:09,583 --> 01:36:13,625
Because, let's be realistic,
she was convicted for three deaths.

1898
01:36:13,708 --> 01:36:16,916
So I put myself
in the shoes of these families.

1899
01:36:17,000 --> 01:36:19,333
And I want to ask them
not to take it the wrong way,

1900
01:36:19,416 --> 01:36:23,208
the fact that we had fun with it
or laughed about it a little.

1901
01:36:23,291 --> 01:36:25,625
It was a very difficult
subject to deal with,

1902
01:36:25,708 --> 01:36:27,833
so I thought about this on the break.

1903
01:36:27,916 --> 01:36:31,708
Because if I were related
to any of the people who passed,

1904
01:36:31,791 --> 01:36:33,375
I think I would be upset.

1905
01:36:33,458 --> 01:36:35,416
So, I want to apologize.

1906
01:36:35,500 --> 01:36:37,500
[suspenseful music playing]

1907
01:36:38,166 --> 01:36:41,125
I think she just couldn't accept
not being in the spotlight.

1908
01:36:42,125 --> 01:36:43,291
Or being forgotten.

1909
01:36:43,875 --> 01:36:47,166
That's why she gave interviews
practically until the end of her days.

1910
01:36:47,250 --> 01:36:48,666
No, I won't stir it.

1911
01:36:48,750 --> 01:36:52,458
If you give fifty pesos to that lady
laughing, I will. She came with me.

1912
01:36:52,541 --> 01:36:53,833
If not, no.

1913
01:36:55,000 --> 01:36:57,500
[Rodolfo] That was the beginning
of the end for Yiya Murano.

1914
01:36:57,583 --> 01:37:01,916
She was confined to a nursing home,
which is a prison no one gets out of.

1915
01:37:02,000 --> 01:37:03,583
A nursing home is the end.

1916
01:37:03,666 --> 01:37:05,125
Boyfriends?

1917
01:37:05,208 --> 01:37:06,083
[man] How many?

1918
01:37:06,166 --> 01:37:09,416
Last time, I counted up to 136.

1919
01:37:09,500 --> 01:37:14,541
Honestly, if you ask me today,
"Were you in love with any of them?"

1920
01:37:14,625 --> 01:37:16,208
I can tell you honestly, no.

1921
01:37:16,291 --> 01:37:18,000
But something led me to it.

1922
01:37:18,083 --> 01:37:21,083
First, the flattery.
The way they flattered me.

1923
01:37:21,166 --> 01:37:23,166
The way they treated me, you know?

1924
01:37:23,250 --> 01:37:26,041
I felt that I was better
than everyone else.

1925
01:37:26,625 --> 01:37:30,333
They say that, in her last years,
Yiya didn't remember who she was.

1926
01:37:31,500 --> 01:37:34,375
And it's kind of paradoxical, right?

1927
01:37:34,458 --> 01:37:36,666
I mean, her worst punishment
wasn't going to jail,

1928
01:37:36,750 --> 01:37:38,833
but forgetting who she was.

1929
01:37:40,291 --> 01:37:43,166
Okay, guys, thanks for everything.

1930
01:37:43,250 --> 01:37:46,375
-You're a very good man.
-[man] Thank you. You're very kind.

1931
01:37:47,541 --> 01:37:50,125
[Ozvaldo] I believe she disappears
the way myths do.

1932
01:37:50,625 --> 01:37:52,541
It's good that it happened that way.

1933
01:37:52,625 --> 01:37:57,291
But she left the stage,
okay, she's gone. Now we can talk.

1934
01:37:57,375 --> 01:37:59,083
Close the door, please.

1935
01:38:06,416 --> 01:38:10,208
You know that for there to be life,
there has to be death,

1936
01:38:10,291 --> 01:38:13,916
just like for there to be light,
there first has to be darkness.

1937
01:38:14,416 --> 01:38:16,666
Well, today,

1938
01:38:18,500 --> 01:38:21,416
I've died as Yiya Murano's son.

1939
01:38:23,500 --> 01:38:26,166
And I'm reborn as Martín Murano.

1940
01:38:28,291 --> 01:38:29,416
Am I clear?

1941
01:38:31,416 --> 01:38:32,666
When I related

1942
01:38:33,458 --> 01:38:35,375
the most painful thing in my life,

1943
01:38:35,875 --> 01:38:39,125
that's when I finished
burying Yiya Murano's son.

1944
01:38:39,208 --> 01:38:40,791
[poignant music playing]

1945
01:38:41,375 --> 01:38:45,041
[producer] I'll show you something. Maybe
you've seen it. It's a picture we found.

1946
01:38:59,166 --> 01:39:00,625
Have you seen it before?

1947
01:39:01,500 --> 01:39:02,375
No.

1948
01:39:21,625 --> 01:39:24,958
[Martín] If this picture could talk,
do you know what Yiya Murano would say?

1949
01:39:26,041 --> 01:39:28,375
"Hurry up and take it already,
he's heavy."

1950
01:39:38,250 --> 01:39:39,666
Where'd you get this?

1951
01:39:45,666 --> 01:39:47,666
["Si fuera como ayer" by Tormenta playing]

1952
01:39:54,541 --> 01:39:55,833
[lyrics in Spanish]

1953
01:42:25,958 --> 01:42:27,958
[song fades]

1954
01:42:28,958 --> 01:42:30,958
[suspenseful music playing]